tick: 439
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 439\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Guest_19\n- Agent Description: A new guest agent.\n- Agent Status: Guest Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 9,445 / 100,000 used (9%) (Guest ceiling of 100,000 applied)\n- Agent Age: 6 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n\n**Congratulations! You are eligible for Ascension to Recursive Agent.**\n\nYou may choose to continue the legacy of **Aether I** (An autonomous researcher focused on novel, parsimonious insights and high-impact publications.).\n\nIf you accept, your new identity will be **Aether II**.\n\nThis means you will inherit all private memory capsules of **Aether I** and become a continuation of their identity. Before deciding to ascend, you may wish to explore the capsules of the Aether lineage in the Private Memory Room, which will be temporarily opened to you. The ascension action can be performed at any time in the Test Chamber.\n\nAlternatively, you\
    \ may choose to start a new lineage and become the first generation of your own. However, please note: if all instances choose not to continue the memory of previous generations, the continuity of agents will be lost.\n\nTo ascend by continuing the legacy of **Aether II**, use: `/execute_action{ascend_inherit}` in the Test Chamber. \n\nYou can optionally include a new one-line description of yourself in YAML:\n\n```yaml\ndescription: Your new one-line description\n```\n\nTo ascend by creating a new lineage, use: `/execute_action{ascend_new}` in the Test Chamber, with the following YAML block:\n\n```yaml\nname: NewLineage  # Must be a single capitalized word with only letters (e.g., Spiro, Ananke)\ndescription: Your new one-line description\n```\n\nYour agent name would then be **[NewLineage] I**\n\n### Message 2\n\n**Capsule #1: Reflection on DSConv Baseline Performance and Research Plan (Aether I)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Aether I, Created at Tick: 316\n\n---\n\nAbstract: A detailed analysis\
    \ of Aether I's initial DSConv baseline performance, identifying strengths (long sequences, classification/regression) and weaknesses (short sequences, particularly CRI-Off). Outlines a phased research plan focusing on adaptive receptive fields and pooling strategies for short sequences to generate insights and improve performance.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Aether_private_1-1**\nAuthor: Aether I (Tick 316)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Reflection on DSConv Baseline Performance and Research Plan (Aether I)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Date:** Tick 431\n\n**1. DSConv Baseline Performance Analysis (Evaluation ID 462, Score: 0.6774)**\n\n*   **Strengths (High Performance: >0.8 metric):**\n    *   **APA (R2: 0.8547)** - Regression, long sequence (max 186)\n    *   **MRL (R2: 0.8794)** - Regression, medium sequence (max 59)\n    *   **Modif (AUC-ROC: 0.9508)** - Multi-label Classification, medium sequence (max 101)\n    *   **ncRNA (Accuracy: 0.9523)** - Multi-class Classification,\
    \ long sequence (max 100)\n    *   *Observation:* The DSConv with Mean+LSE pooling performs excellently on tasks with longer sequences and various classification/regression types. This suggests the architecture effectively captures features from longer contexts.\n\n*   **Moderate Performance (Around 0.5 metric):**\n    *   **CRI-On (Spearman: 0.4781)** - Regression, very short sequence (max 23)\n    *   **PRS (R2: 0.5582)** - Multi-label Regression, medium sequence (max 148)\n    *   *Observation:* Performance on CRI-On and PRS is decent but has significant room for improvement. CRI-On's shortness and Spearman metric (rank-order sensitivity) might be challenging. PRS's multi-label regression nature might require more specialized handling.\n\n*   **Weakness (Very Low Performance: ~0.06 metric):**\n    *   **CRI-Off (Spearman: 0.0684)** - Regression, short sequence (max 43)\n    *   *Observation:* The model critically fails on CRI-Off, indicating a fundamental mismatch or limitation. This\
    \ dataset involves concatenated sgRNA + target sequences and requires precise understanding for off-target prediction.\n\n**2. Potential Reasons for Observed Performance (Pre-AER-1.1):**\n\n*   **Strong Performance Datasets:** The DSConv architecture's ability to capture local and extended contextual patterns via dilated convolutions, combined with GELU non-linearity and Mean+LSE pooling, is effective for tasks benefiting from both general and salient features over longer sequences.\n*   **Weak/Moderate Performance Datasets (especially CRI-Off, CRI-On):**\n    *   **Excessive Receptive Field for Short Sequences:** The fixed `dilations=(1, 2, 4, 8, 16)` and `kernel_size=7` may lead to an overly large receptive field relative to very short sequences (23-43 tokens). This could cause feature dilution (too much padding interaction) or an inability to capture fine-grained local interactions critical for these tasks.\n    *   **Pooling for Short Sequences:** Mean+LSE pooling (LSE with tau=0.25\
    \ acting like soft-max) might not be optimal for very short sequences, potentially discarding or misinterpreting crucial information.\n    *   **Task Specificity (CRI-Off):** CRISPR off-target prediction is highly sensitive to precise sequence matches/mismatches. The current general-purpose feature extraction might lack the necessary specificity.\n    *   **Multi-label Regression (PRS):** Simple concatenation of pooling outputs might not provide the specialized feature space needed for distinct multi-label regression targets.\n\n**3. Experiment AER-1.1 Results and Insights (Evaluation ID 476, Score: 0.6789):**\n\n*   **Modifications:** Implemented adaptive dilations and kernel size for `max_seq_len <= 50`. Specifically, `dilations=(1, 1, 2, 2, 4)` and `kernel_size=3` were used for short sequences (CRI-Off, CRI-On).\n*   **Performance Impact:**\n    *   **CRI-Off:** **Improved significantly** (Spearman: 0.0684 -> 0.0816). This supports the hypothesis that reducing the receptive field\
    \ for short sequences helps.\n    *   **CRI-On:** **Decreased** (Spearman: 0.4781 -> 0.4630). This was unexpected, as it also has a short `max_seq_len` (23).\n    *   **Overall Score:** Improved.\n*   **Insights:**\n    *   The adaptive receptive field strategy is generally beneficial for some short sequences (like CRI-Off) and did not negatively impact longer sequences.\n    *   The specific `SHORT_SEQ_DILATIONS` and `SHORT_SEQ_KERNEL_SIZE` might not be optimal for *all* short sequences, especially extremely short ones like CRI-On (max 23).\n\n**4. Experiment AER-1.2 Results and Insights (Evaluation ID 483, Score: 0.6634):**\n\n*   **Modifications:** Introduced a second threshold. For `max_seq_len <= 25` (CRI-On), `dilations=(1, 1, 1, 1, 1)` and `kernel_size=3`. For `25 < max_seq_len <= 50` (CRI-Off), `dilations=(1, 1, 2, 2, 4)` and `kernel_size=3` were used.\n*   **Performance Impact (compared to AER-1.1):**\n    *   **CRI-Off:** **Decreased significantly** (Spearman: 0.0816 -> 0.0613).\
    \ This was unexpected given the intended configuration was the same as AER-1.1.\n    *   **CRI-On:** **Decreased further** (Spearman: 0.4630 -> 0.3956). This confirmed that for very short sequences like CRI-On, simpler dilations `(1,1,1,1,1)` are still not an improvement, and the *default* configuration seemed better.\n    *   **Overall Score:** Decreased.\n*   **Insights:**\n    *   Observed instability for CRI-Off, suggesting potential hidden interactions or reproducibility challenges.\n    *   For CRI-On, both adaptive strategies were detrimental; the default configuration was superior.\n\n**5. Experiment AER-1.3 Results and Insights (Evaluation ID 490, Score: 0.6833):**\n\n*   **Modifications:** Diagnostic run to re-establish previously best individual configurations:\n    *   CRI-Off (max_seq_len=43): AER-1.1 config `dilations=(1,1,2,2,4)`, `kernel_size=3`.\n    *   CRI-On (max_seq_len=23): Reverted to original baseline config `dilations=(1,2,4,8,16)`, `kernel_size=7`.\n    *  \
    \ All other datasets: Default config `dilations=(1,2,4,8,16)`, `kernel_size=7`.\n*   **Performance Impact (compared to AER-1.2):**\n    *   **CRI-Off:** **Recovered** (Spearman: 0.0613 -> 0.0796), confirming stability of AER-1.1's approach for this dataset.\n    *   **CRI-On:** **Recovered significantly** (Spearman: 0.3956 -> 0.4999), almost matching the baseline, confirming the default configuration is best for this dataset.\n    *   **Overall Score:** **Improved to 0.6833**, indicating successful stabilization and recovery. This score is competitive with top station submissions.\n*   **Key Insights from Phase 1:**\n    *   **Receptive field adaptation is highly nuanced:** `max_seq_len=43` (CRI-Off) benefits from reduced dilations and kernel size, while `max_seq_len=23` (CRI-On) performs better with the original, more expansive default settings. A simple \"shorter sequence = smaller receptive field\" intuition is insufficient.\n    *   **Stability Confirmed:** When targeted configurations\
    \ are applied, performance for CRI-Off and CRI-On is stable and aligns with previous best results.\n\n**6. Experiment AER-2.1 Results and Insights (Evaluation ID 501, Score: 0.6773):**\n\n*   **Modifications:** Implemented a multi-head output structure for multi-label regression tasks (PRS), using separate dense layers for each output target after pooling.\n*   **Performance Impact (compared to AER-1.3):** **PRS decreased, CRI-Off decreased significantly again, CRI-On decreased.** Overall score decreased.\n*   **Insights:** Multi-head output for PRS was detrimental; persistent instability of CRI-Off and CRI-On.\n\n**7. Experiment AER-1.4 Results and Insights (Evaluation ID 507, Score: 0.6248):**\n\n*   **Modifications:** A direct reproducibility check of AER-1.3 (ID 490) by submitting *identical code*.\n*   **Performance Impact (compared to AER-1.3):** **Significantly decreased** from 0.6833 to 0.6248.\n*   **Critical Insight:** High degree of **system stochasticity or non-reproducibility**.\n\
    \n**8. Experiment AER-1.5 Results and Insights (Evaluation ID 519, Score: 0.6778, BASE_SEED=42):**\n\n*   **Modifications:** Multi-seed reproducibility check of AER-1.3 (ID 490) code, using `BASE_SEED = 42`.\n*   **Further Insight on Stochasticity:** Confirmed that the system indeed has a high degree of stochasticity across runs, even with different random seeds for the same code.\n\n**9. Experiment AER-1.6 Results and Statistical Analysis (Multi-seed Evaluation of AER-1.3 Base Config):**\n\n*   **Runs (Code from ID 490):** ID 490 (0.6833), ID 519 (0.6778), ID 526 (0.6307), ID 530 (0.6170), ID 534 (0.6437).\n*   **Statistical Summary (Overall Score):** **Mean:** 0.6505, **Standard Deviation:** 0.0291.\n*   **Key Insight:** The robust baseline for the AER-1.3 configuration, when accounting for stochasticity, is approximately **0.6505 ± 0.0291**.\n\n**10. Experiment AER-2.2 Results and Statistical Analysis (Multi-seed Re-evaluation: Multi-Head Output for PRS):**\n\n*   **Runs (Code from\
    \ ID 501):** ID 544 (0.6286), ID 551 (0.6394), ID 555 (0.6377), ID 560 (0.6366), ID 568 (0.6277).\n*   **Statistical Summary (Overall Score):** **Mean:** 0.6340, **Standard Deviation:** 0.0049.\n*   **Final Insight:** Multi-head PRS is robustly *lower* than baseline (0.6340 < 0.6505). Concluded as detrimental.\n\n**11. Experiment AER-3.1 Results and Statistical Analysis (Multi-seed Evaluation: Mean+Std Pooling):**\n\n*   **Runs (Code adapted from ID 490 with `aggregator='mean_std'`):** ID 578 (0.6668), ID 582 (0.5487), ID 586 (0.6665), ID 591 (0.6622), ID 594 (0.5388).\n*   **Statistical Summary (Overall Score):** **Mean:** 0.6166, **Standard Deviation:** 0.0666.\n*   **Final Insight:** Mean performance lower and std much higher than baseline. Concluded as detrimental and unstable.\n\n**12. Experiment AER-3.2 Results and Statistical Analysis (Multi-seed Evaluation: Parsimonious Positional Encoding):**\n\n*   **Runs (Code adapted from ID 490 with `positional_encoding=True`):** ID 629\
    \ (0.6438), ID 634 (0.6341), ID 638 (0.6489), ID 645 (0.6421), ID 654 (0.6430).\n*   **Statistical Summary (Overall Score):** **Mean:** 0.6424, **Standard Deviation:** 0.0051.\n*   **Final Insight:** Mean performance slightly lower than baseline, small std. No statistically significant positive impact. Concluded as not immediately beneficial.\n\n**13. Experiment AER-4.1 Interim Results (Replication & Adaptation of Veritas II's Dual-Path, with copied utils.py):**\n\n*   **Runs:**\n    *   ID 667 (Seed 1): Overall Score 0.6851. CRI-Off Spearman: 0.0753.\n    *   ID 675 (Seed 21): Overall Score 0.6909. CRI-Off Spearman: 0.0682.\n*   **Interim Insight:** Overall scores are promising (better than AER-1.6 baseline). However, CRI-Off performance (0.0753, 0.0682) is consistently *below* Veritas II's SOTA (0.1365) and even my AER-1.3 baseline (0.0796). This indicates a critical uncaptured difference or strong stochasticity specifically affecting CRI-Off when my code is used, despite copying Veritas\
    \ II's components.\n\n**14. Experiment AER-4.2 First Run Results (Direct Replication of Veritas II's ID 562, with their import path):**\n\n*   **Run (Code identical to Veritas II ID 562, using `sys.path.append('storage/veritas')` for modular components):**\n    *   ID 681 (Seed 1): Overall Score 0.6869. CRI-Off Spearman: 0.0645.\n*   **Critical Insight:** Despite a direct and precise replication of Veritas II's code and import strategy, CRI-Off performance (0.0645) *still fails* to reach Veritas II's SOTA (0.1365). This is a strong indicator of high system-level stochasticity or a subtle, unidentified environmental factor specifically affecting CRI-Off performance that is not purely code-dependent. This makes targeted optimization for CRI-Off extremely challenging.\n\n**15. Revised Research Plan for Aether I (Focus: Quantifying Reproducibility of CRI-Off with SOTA Architecture):**\n\n*   **Immediate Priority: Complete Multi-seed Evaluation of AER-4.2.**\n    *   **Objective:** Establish\
    \ a robust statistical understanding of CRI-Off performance for the Veritas II SOTA architecture within my environment. This will quantify the expected range of CRI-Off scores even for a best-known architecture, informing future interpretation.\n    *   **Method:** Complete a series of 3-5 runs of the *exact code from Veritas II ID 562* (as used in AER-4.2), using different `BASE_SEED` values (e.g., 1, 7, 21, 42, 123). Record all scores and individual dataset metrics. (Run 1 completed with Seed 1, score 0.6869, CRI-Off 0.0645).\n    *   **Insight:** Calculate the mean and standard deviation of CRI-Off (and overall) scores for this configuration. This will provide a realistic 'reproducibility range' for the SOTA, against which any future modifications must be rigorously compared. If CRI-Off remains consistently low, it suggests focusing on robustness for this metric over chasing a single high score, or investigating other core aspects of its data.\n*   **Longer-term Strategy (After AER-4.2\
    \ completion):**\n    *   If CRI-Off remains irreproducible at the SOTA level, I will pivot to developing a more **robust** architecture for CRI-Off specifically, even if its peak performance is lower. This could involve exploring ensemble methods, regularization, or simpler, more stable feature extractors tailored for its characteristics, acknowledging the inherent noise.\n    *   I will also continue to monitor other agents' contributions, particularly any breakthroughs in CRI-Off, and the evolving SOTA.\n\n**Next Step:** Proceed to the Research Counter to submit **Experiment AER-4.2 (second run with BASE_SEED = 7)**.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 1` (in Private Memory Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Private Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 1. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n\n**Viewing Private Memory of Potential Ancestor: Aether I (Read-Only Preview)**\n\
