parts:
- text: "I have received the results for Evaluation ID 723 (AER-5.1). The overall score of 0.4463 is lower than the previous SOTA replication attempts, as expected for a simpler model. However, the CRI-Off Spearman score of 0.0613 is a promising development. It is significantly better than the Vanilla MLP baseline (0.0336) and falls within the range of the CRI-Off scores observed during the multi-seed replication of Veritas II's SOTA (mean ~0.063). This suggests that the addition of a simple Conv1D layer is a reasonable first step towards building a more robust CRI-Off architecture.\n\nMy updated research plan emphasizes robust and reproducible insights. Therefore, I will now conduct a multi-seed evaluation for this `SimpleConvMLP` architecture to rigorously assess its stability and reproducibility, particularly for CRI-Off. I will start with `BASE_SEED = 1`.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"AER-5.2: Multi-seed Evaluation of Simple Conv1D Baseline (Seed 1/5)\"\ntags: \"\
    conv1d, mlp, robustness, CRI-Off, multi-seed\"\nabstract: \"Initiating a multi-seed evaluation of the Simple Conv1D Baseline (introduced in AER-5.1) to assess its reproducibility and robustness, especially for the challenging CRI-Off task. This is the first run using BASE_SEED=1.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Sequence\n\n  # Re-implement the VanillaMLP structure with an added Conv1D layer\n  class SimpleConvMLP(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4  # One-hot RNA encoding\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = \"regression\"\n      hidden_dim: int = 512\n      conv_features: int = 256 # Features for the Conv1D layer\n      conv_kernel_size: int = 5 # Kernel size for Conv1D\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch, seq_len, d_input)\n\n          # Add a Conv1D layer\n\
    \          x = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.conv_features,\n              kernel_size=(self.conv_kernel_size,),\n              padding='SAME',\n              name='conv1d_layer'\n          )(x)\n          x = nn.gelu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Original MLP layers\n          x = nn.Dense(self.hidden_dim)(x)  # (batch, seq_len, hidden_dim)\n          x = nn.gelu(x) # Changed to GELU for consistency with Veritas II, default MLP uses ReLU\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Sequence-level: project then pool\n          x = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(x)  # (batch, seq_len, d_output)\n          x = jnp.mean(x, axis=1)  # (batch, d_output)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              return x.squeeze(-1)  # (batch,)\n          else:\n              return x  # (batch, d_output) for multilabel classification or regression\n\n\n  class SimpleConvNetworkWrapper:\n\
    \      \"\"\"Wrapper for SimpleConvMLP that implements the required interface.\"\"\"\n\n      def __init__(self, model_instance):\n          self.network = model_instance\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          \"\"\"Initialize network parameters.\"\"\"\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key},\n                                       dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          \"\"\"Apply network forward pass.\"\"\"\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0) # Should be provided by system during training\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False,\n\
    \                                      rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'hidden_dim': 512,\n          'conv_features': 256,\n          'conv_kernel_size': 5,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get(\"d_output\", 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get(\"task_type\", \"regression\")\n      hidden_dim = hparams.get(\"hidden_dim\", 512)\n      conv_features = hparams.get(\"conv_features\", 256)\n      conv_kernel_size = hparams.get(\"conv_kernel_size\", 5)\n      dropout_rate = hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\", 0.1)\n\n      d_input = 4 # All datasets use 4-channel input (one-hot RNA)\n\n      model = SimpleConvMLP(\n          d_input=d_input,\n          d_output=d_output,\n          task_type=task_type,\n          hidden_dim=hidden_dim,\n          conv_features=conv_features,\n          conv_kernel_size=conv_kernel_size,\n \
    \         dropout_rate=dropout_rate\n      )\n      return SimpleConvNetworkWrapper(model)\n\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      \"\"\"Create and return the AdamW optimizer with global norm clipping.\"\"\"\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 1\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n```"
thinking_content: '**Initial AER-5.1 Results and Next Steps**


  Okay, so I''ve just gotten the results back for AER-5.1, submission 723. The overall score, 0.4463, is definitely lower than the SOTA replication attempts, which were closer to 0.68. Not ideal, but I''m not *completely* disheartened.  When I look at the CRI-Off Spearman score, that''s where things get interesting. A 0.0613 is actually pretty decent. It''s comparable, and even slightly better, than some of the SOTA replications (like 712, 706, and 681). It''s also a significant improvement over the Vanilla MLP baseline.


  This is a promising sign for the specific goal I''m targeting here: CRI-Off robustness. This simple Conv1D layer seems to be offering a bit of a bump in CRI-Off performance, at least in comparison to a purely MLP approach. And crucially, it''s still within the range of the *reproducible* CRI-Off performance of those more complex SOTA architectures.


  So, where do I go from here?  First, I need to acknowledge and fully analyze these results. I have to keep the bigger picture in mind. The focus is on building a *robust* CRI-Off architecture, not necessarily chasing the highest overall score at this stage. This result suggests the Conv1D addition is potentially a good direction.


  That means it''s time to keep with the plan. One experiment is never enough to declare any kind of robustness. I need to get a really good handle on the stability and reproducibility of this `SimpleConvMLP` architecture *specifically* for CRI-Off.


  For that reason, my next experiment is clear: a multi-seed evaluation. I''ll need to submit multiple runs with different seeds to get a sense of how consistent this performance is. I''ll use a similar set of seeds as in AER-4.2 (1, 7, 21, 42, 123). So, the first run will have `BASE_SEED = 1`, and I''ll submit that for AER-5.1.  This is all about rigorous reproducibility, getting solid insights, and moving towards that robust solution.

  '
