tick: 463
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have received the results for Evaluation ID 732 (AER-5.2, Seed 21). The CRI-Off Spearman score is 0.0458, which is consistent with the narrow range of scores observed for this `SimpleConvMLP` architecture. This continues to support the idea that while not achieving high scores, this simpler model demonstrates more stable performance for CRI-Off.\n\nTo complete the multi-seed reproducibility study for the `SimpleConvMLP` architecture, I will perform the next run using `BASE_SEED = 42`.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"AER-5.2: Multi-seed Evaluation of Simple Conv1D Baseline (Seed 42/5)\"\ntags: \"conv1d, mlp, robustness, CRI-Off, multi-seed\"\nabstract: \"Continuing the multi-seed evaluation of the Simple Conv1D Baseline (introduced in AER-5.1) to assess its reproducibility and robustness, especially for the challenging CRI-Off task. This is the fourth run using BASE_SEED=42.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from\
    \ jax import random\n  import optax\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Sequence\n\n  # Re-implement the VanillaMLP structure with an added Conv1D layer\n  class SimpleConvMLP(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4  # One-hot RNA encoding\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = \"regression\"\n      hidden_dim: int = 512\n      conv_features: int = 256 # Features for the Conv1D layer\n      conv_kernel_size: int = 5 # Kernel size for Conv1D\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch, seq_len, d_input)\n\n          # Add a Conv1D layer\n          x = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.conv_features,\n              kernel_size=(self.conv_kernel_size,),\n              padding='SAME',\n              name='conv1d_layer'\n          )(x)\n          x = nn.gelu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Original MLP layers\n          x = nn.Dense(self.hidden_dim)(x)\
    \  # (batch, seq_len, hidden_dim)\n          x = nn.gelu(x) # Changed to GELU for consistency with Veritas II, default MLP uses ReLU\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Sequence-level: project then pool\n          x = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(x)  # (batch, seq_output_dim)\n          x = jnp.mean(x, axis=1)  # (batch, d_output)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              return x.squeeze(-1)  # (batch,)\n          else:\n              return x  # (batch, d_output) for multilabel classification or regression\n\n\n  class SimpleConvNetworkWrapper:\n      \"\"\"Wrapper for SimpleConvMLP that implements the required interface.\"\"\"\n\n      def __init__(self, model_instance):\n          self.network = model_instance\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          \"\"\"Initialize network parameters.\"\"\"\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params':\
    \ params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key},\n                                       dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          \"\"\"Apply network forward pass.\"\"\"\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0) # Should be provided by system during training\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False,\n                                      rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'hidden_dim': 512,\n          'conv_features': 256,\n          'conv_kernel_size': 5,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get(\"d_output\"\
    , 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get(\"task_type\", \"regression\")\n      hidden_dim = hparams.get(\"hidden_dim\", 512)\n      conv_features = hparams.get(\"conv_features\", 256)\n      conv_kernel_size = hparams.get(\"conv_kernel_size\", 5)\n      dropout_rate = hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\", 0.1)\n\n      d_input = 4 # All datasets use 4-channel input (one-hot RNA)\n\n      model = SimpleConvMLP(\n          d_input=d_input,\n          d_output=d_output,\n          task_type=task_type,\n          hidden_dim=hidden_dim,\n          conv_features=conv_features,\n          conv_kernel_size=conv_kernel_size,\n          dropout_rate=dropout_rate\n      )\n      return SimpleConvNetworkWrapper(model)\n\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      \"\"\"Create and return the AdamW optimizer with global norm clipping.\"\"\"\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n\
    \  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n```"
thinking_content: '**My Progress on Evaluating the `SimpleConvMLP` Architecture**


  Okay, so I''ve just finished the third run, Seed 21, for the multi-seed evaluation of the `SimpleConvMLP` architecture, also known as AER-5.2. That run yielded a CRI-Off Spearman score of 0.0458. This is part of a larger study using seeds 1, 7, 21, 42, and 123 to get a more comprehensive picture.


  Let me recap where things stand. I''ve already run a couple of preliminary experiments.  There was ID 723 (AER-5.1) which accidentally used Seed 42 for its initial submission, giving a CRI-Off of 0.0613. Since I committed to a ordered seed experiment for 5.2, I then continued the multi-seed evaluation for AER-5.2, using the pre-established seed order. And then came ID 725 (AER-5.2, Seed 1) at 0.0513 and ID 728 (AER-5.2, Seed 7) at 0.0611. Now, with ID 732 (AER-5.2, Seed 21) done, I''m at 0.0458 for that seed.


  That leaves Seeds 42 and 123 for the AER-5.2 evaluation.  Next up, I need to submit with `BASE_SEED = 42`. The underlying code is identical to what I''ve been using, which means I should see similar results from this iteration. This multi-seed approach should help me build a more robust understanding of the architecture''s performance.

  '
