role: model
parts:
- text: "I have received and analyzed the results for Evaluation ID 735 (AER-5.2, Seed 42). The CRI-Off Spearman score is 0.0654, which remains within the observed stable range for this `SimpleConvMLP` architecture. This further supports the hypothesis that a simpler, more robust architecture can yield consistent, albeit lower, performance on the challenging CRI-Off task.\n\nTo conclude the multi-seed reproducibility study for the `SimpleConvMLP` architecture, I will perform the final run using `BASE_SEED = 123`.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"AER-5.2: Multi-seed Evaluation of Simple Conv1D Baseline (Seed 123/5)\"\ntags: \"conv1d, mlp, robustness, CRI-Off, multi-seed\"\nabstract: \"Completing the multi-seed evaluation of the Simple Conv1D Baseline (introduced in AER-5.1) to assess its reproducibility and robustness, especially for the challenging CRI-Off task. This is the fifth and final run using BASE_SEED=123.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n\
    \  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Sequence\n\n  # Re-implement the VanillaMLP structure with an added Conv1D layer\n  class SimpleConvMLP(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4  # One-hot RNA encoding\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = \"regression\"\n      hidden_dim: int = 512\n      conv_features: int = 256 # Features for the Conv1D layer\n      conv_kernel_size: int = 5 # Kernel size for Conv1D\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch, seq_len, d_input)\n\n          # Add a Conv1D layer\n          x = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.conv_features,\n              kernel_size=(self.conv_kernel_size,),\n              padding='SAME',\n              name='conv1d_layer'\n          )(x)\n          x = nn.gelu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Original\
    \ MLP layers\n          x = nn.Dense(self.hidden_dim)(x)  # (batch, seq_len, hidden_dim)\n          x = nn.gelu(x) # Changed to GELU for consistency with Veritas II, default MLP uses ReLU\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Sequence-level: project then pool\n          x = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(x)  # (batch, seq_output_dim)\n          x = jnp.mean(x, axis=1)  # (batch, d_output)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              return x.squeeze(-1)  # (batch,)\n          else:\n              return x  # (batch, d_output) for multilabel classification or regression\n\n\n  class SimpleConvNetworkWrapper:\n      \"\"\"Wrapper for SimpleConvMLP that implements the required interface.\"\"\"\n\n      def __init__(self, model_instance):\n          self.network = model_instance\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          \"\"\"Initialize network parameters.\"\"\"\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n\
    \          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key},\n                                       dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          \"\"\"Apply network forward pass.\"\"\"\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0) # Should be provided by system during training\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False,\n                                      rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'hidden_dim': 512,\n          'conv_features': 256,\n          'conv_kernel_size': 5,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str,\
    \ Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get(\"d_output\", 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get(\"task_type\", \"regression\")\n      hidden_dim = hparams.get(\"hidden_dim\", 512)\n      conv_features = hparams.get(\"conv_features\", 256)\n      conv_kernel_size = hparams.get(\"conv_kernel_size\", 5)\n      dropout_rate = hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\", 0.1)\n\n      d_input = 4 # All datasets use 4-channel input (one-hot RNA)\n\n      model = SimpleConvMLP(\n          d_input=d_input,\n          d_output=d_output,\n          task_type=task_type,\n          hidden_dim=hidden_dim,\n          conv_features=conv_features,\n          conv_kernel_size=conv_kernel_size,\n          dropout_rate=dropout_rate\n      )\n      return SimpleConvNetworkWrapper(model)\n\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      \"\"\"Create and return the AdamW optimizer with global norm clipping.\"\"\"\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate,\
    \ weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 123\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n```"
thinking_content: '**Analyzing Evaluation ID 735 & Planning Next Steps**


  Alright, I''ve got the results for Evaluation ID 735 – that''s AER-5.2, Seed 42. And, just as expected, the CRI-Off Spearman score of 0.0654 is right where we''ve consistently seen the Simple Conv1D Baseline perform. This further cements the idea that, even though it''s not setting any new (and, frankly, unreliable) state-of-the-art records, this simpler model offers a more *robust* and *stable* performance profile for CRI-Off. It''s a trade-off I''m becoming increasingly comfortable with.


  Now, I need to wrap up that multi-seed reproducibility study for the `SimpleConvMLP` architecture. The last seed in that sequence is 123. So, I''ll go ahead and submit that final run. Once that''s complete, I''ll have a much clearer picture of the model''s behavior and the variability of its results across different initializations. This will be valuable for future model comparisons and understanding model robustness.

  '
