tick: 465
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have received the results for Evaluation ID 737, marking the completion of the multi-seed reproducibility study for the `SimpleConvMLP` architecture.\n\nThe CRI-Off Spearman scores from the 6 runs of AER-5.1 (initial) and AER-5.2 (multi-seed) are:\n[0.0613 (ID 723), 0.0513 (ID 725), 0.0611 (ID 728), 0.0458 (ID 732), 0.0654 (ID 735), 0.0640 (ID 737)].\nThe mean CRI-Off Spearman score is approximately **0.05815**, with a standard deviation of approximately **0.0071**.\n\nThis demonstrates that the `SimpleConvMLP` consistently performs within a stable range for CRI-Off, outperforming the Vanilla MLP baseline (0.0336), and maintaining scores comparable to the *achieved* non-reproducible range of the SOTA replications (mean ~0.063, but with much higher variance for the SOTA). This suggests that a simpler architecture can indeed offer more robust, if not peak, performance for CRI-Off.\n\nFollowing my updated research plan to develop a more **robust** architecture for CRI-Off, I will\
    \ now refine this `SimpleConvMLP`. My next experiment (AER-5.3) will investigate the impact of a smaller convolutional kernel size. Given CRI-Off's relatively short sequence length (max 43), a smaller kernel might capture more fine-grained local features crucial for this task. I will set `conv_kernel_size = 3` and use `BASE_SEED = 42`.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"AER-5.3: Simple Conv1D Baseline with Smaller Kernel Size (Kernel 3)\"\ntags: \"conv1d, mlp, robustness, CRI-Off, kernel_size\"\nabstract: \"Refining the Simple Conv1D Baseline (AER-5.1/5.2), this experiment explores the impact of a reduced convolutional kernel size (`conv_kernel_size=3`) to potentially capture more fine-grained local features, aiming for further improved robustness and performance specifically for CRI-Off.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Sequence\n\n  class SimpleConvMLP(nn.Module):\n\
    \      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = \"regression\"\n      hidden_dim: int = 512\n      conv_features: int = 256\n      conv_kernel_size: int = 3 # MODIFIED: Changed kernel size to 3\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          x = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.conv_features,\n              kernel_size=(self.conv_kernel_size,),\n              padding='SAME',\n              name='conv1d_layer'\n          )(x)\n          x = nn.gelu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          x = nn.Dense(self.hidden_dim)(x)\n          x = nn.gelu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          x = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(x)\n          x = jnp.mean(x, axis=1)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              return x.squeeze(-1)\n          else:\n              return x\n\n\n\
    \  class SimpleConvNetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, model_instance):\n          self.network = model_instance\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key},\n                                       dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False,\n                                      rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'hidden_dim': 512,\n          'conv_features':\
    \ 256,\n          'conv_kernel_size': 3, # MODIFIED\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get(\"d_output\", 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get(\"task_type\", \"regression\")\n      hidden_dim = hparams.get(\"hidden_dim\", 512)\n      conv_features = hparams.get(\"conv_features\", 256)\n      conv_kernel_size = hparams.get(\"conv_kernel_size\", 3) # MODIFIED\n      dropout_rate = hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\", 0.1)\n\n      d_input = 4\n\n      model = SimpleConvMLP(\n          d_input=d_input,\n          d_output=d_output,\n          task_type=task_type,\n          hidden_dim=hidden_dim,\n          conv_features=conv_features,\n          conv_kernel_size=conv_kernel_size,\n          dropout_rate=dropout_rate\n      )\n      return SimpleConvNetworkWrapper(model)\n\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate,\
    \ weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n```"
thinking_content: '**My Analysis of SimpleConvMLP Reproducibility**


  Alright, I''ve just finished the final run of the multi-seed evaluation for the `SimpleConvMLP` architecture (AER-5.2, Seed 123, ID 737), and it''s time to crunch the numbers and assess the CRI-Off performance for this reproducibility study.  My focus is on understanding the robustness of this architecture and how it compares to our previous findings.


  First things first, I pulled the CRI-Off scores from all the relevant AER-5.2 runs:  ID 725, 728, 732, 735 and 737 and also considered the initial experiment with ID 723 (AER-5.1, implicitly Seed 42).  I calculated the mean and standard deviation to get a handle on the stability of the architecture''s performance. The raw CRI-Off scores were: 0.0613, 0.0513, 0.0611, 0.0458, 0.0654, and 0.0640.  After performing the calculations, the mean CRI-Off performance comes out to roughly 0.05815 with a standard deviation of about 0.0071.


  Now, I need to put this in context.  How does this `SimpleConvMLP` stack up against our previous baselines and state-of-the-art replications? The Vanilla MLP baseline clocked in at 0.0336, and the Veritas II SOTA replications hovered around a mean of 0.063.  While it''s not as good as the vanilla MLP, this average CRI-Off of ~0.058 is better, and crucially, the standard deviation is relatively small, showing decent robustness.  This suggests that even a very simple convolutional layer provides a valuable improvement to the base MLP architecture, at least in this setting. It looks like it is a stable baseline to start from.


  Considering the "Principles of Inquiry" – parsimony and insightful generalizations – the next logical step is to refine this `SimpleConvMLP`.  I''m thinking of a few targeted modifications:  I''ll play around with the `conv_kernel_size` to see if a different receptive field improves performance, perhaps trying a smaller kernel size for these short CRI-Off sequences.  I might tweak the number of `conv_features` or `hidden_dim` as well, but smaller sequences might be better with smaller kernels to start. Adding more layers or different pooling strategies might also be useful. Given the short sequence length of the CRI-Off data (max 43), I suspect larger kernels or many layers might be counterproductive, so the kernel size is a good place to start. I''m going to start by running with `kernel_size = 3` and see what happens.


  Finally, I need to update the submission title and abstract to reflect these new experimental findings.

  '
