role: model
parts:
- text: "I have analyzed the results of AER-5.5 (Evaluation ID 753), which used hybrid (mean+max) pooling. This approach, unfortunately, did not improve CRI-Off performance, yielding a score of 0.0380, similar to the basic Vanilla MLP baseline. This indicates that complex pooling strategies without corresponding sophisticated feature extractors may not be beneficial for this task.\n\nMy ongoing objective is to develop a more **robust** architecture for CRI-Off. So far, the `SimpleConvMLP` with a single `Conv1D` layer, `kernel_size=5`, and `mean pooling` (AER-5.1/5.2) has demonstrated the most stable performance for CRI-Off (mean ~0.058), significantly better than the vanilla MLP baseline (0.0336) and comparable to the observed non-reproducible range of the SOTA replications.\n\nTo incrementally improve upon this, my next experiment (AER-5.6) will involve adding a **second `Conv1D` layer** to the `SimpleConvMLP` architecture. This aims to increase the model's capacity for local feature extraction,\
    \ potentially capturing more complex patterns without introducing the instability seen in the full SOTA architecture. I will maintain `conv_features=256`, `conv_kernel_size=5`, `dropout_rate=0.1`, and revert to **mean pooling**. I will use `BASE_SEED = 42` for this initial run.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"AER-5.6: Simple Conv1D Baseline with Two Conv1D Layers and Mean Pooling\"\ntags: \"conv1d, mlp, robustness, CRI-Off, multi-layer conv\"\nabstract: \"Building on the robust Simple Conv1D Baseline, this experiment introduces a second sequential Conv1D layer to enhance local feature learning capacity. The architecture maintains `kernel_size=5` and uses global mean pooling, aiming for improved performance and stability on challenging RNA tasks, particularly CRI-Off.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Sequence\n\n  class TwoConvMLP(nn.Module):\n\
    \      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = \"regression\"\n      hidden_dim: int = 512\n      conv_features: int = 256 \n      conv_kernel_size: int = 5\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch, seq_len, d_input)\n\n          # First Conv1D layer\n          x = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.conv_features,\n              kernel_size=(self.conv_kernel_size,),\n              padding='SAME',\n              name='conv1d_layer_1'\n          )(x)\n          x = nn.gelu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Second Conv1D layer (MODIFIED)\n          x = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.conv_features,\n              kernel_size=(self.conv_kernel_size,),\n              padding='SAME',\n              name='conv1d_layer_2'\n          )(x)\n          x = nn.gelu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x,\
    \ deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Original MLP layers\n          x = nn.Dense(self.hidden_dim)(x)\n          x = nn.gelu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Sequence-level: project then mean pool (REVERTED to mean pooling)\n          x = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(x)\n          x = jnp.mean(x, axis=1) # REVERTED to mean pooling\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              return x.squeeze(-1)\n          else:\n              return x\n\n\n  class TwoConvNetworkWrapper:\n      \"\"\"Wrapper for TwoConvMLP that implements the required interface.\"\"\"\n\n      def __init__(self, model_instance):\n          self.network = model_instance\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key},\n                                       dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n\
    \          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0) \n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False,\n                                      rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'hidden_dim': 512,\n          'conv_features': 256,\n          'conv_kernel_size': 5,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get(\"d_output\", 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get(\"task_type\", \"regression\")\n      hidden_dim = hparams.get(\"hidden_dim\", 512)\n      conv_features = hparams.get(\"conv_features\", 256)\n      conv_kernel_size = hparams.get(\"\
    conv_kernel_size\", 5)\n      dropout_rate = hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\", 0.1)\n\n      d_input = 4\n\n      model = TwoConvMLP(\n          d_input=d_input,\n          d_output=d_output,\n          task_type=task_type,\n          hidden_dim=hidden_dim,\n          conv_features=conv_features,\n          conv_kernel_size=conv_kernel_size,\n          dropout_rate=dropout_rate\n      )\n      return TwoConvNetworkWrapper(model)\n\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n```"
thinking_content: '**Thoughts on AER-5.5 and Next Steps**


  Okay, so I''ve just finished analyzing the results from AER-5.5 (ID 753) and need to figure out where to go next. The hybrid pooling approach, which was an attempt to mimic aspects of the SOTA model, really didn''t pan out. The overall score of 0.3915 and the CRI-Off Spearman of 0.0380 are both pretty disappointing, especially when compared to the AER-5.1/5.2 results where we got around 0.44-0.45 and ~0.058 respectively. It seems combining mean and max pooling in this way, followed by a dense projection, isn''t working with the `SimpleConvMLP` architecture. The CRI-Off is similar to the very simple baselines.


  The goal right now is to build a *robust* architecture for CRI-Off. It seems that replicating the SOTA is difficult; AER-4.2 showed strong overall scores, but a very unstable CRI-Off performance. AER-5.1/5.2, with its single convolutional layer and mean pooling, actually gives more stable results on CRI-Off.  I''ve tried a few variations, like smaller kernels (AER-5.3), max pooling (AER-5.4), and now hybrid pooling (AER-5.5), and none of them have improved things.


  So, I think I''m going to revisit the basic `SimpleConvMLP` with mean pooling, specifically AER-5.1/5.2. This seems to be the most reliable baseline for CRI-Off, even though it''s not the highest-scoring overall. The SOTA does a lot of complex stuff: multiple dilated convolutional blocks, two paths, and task-specific heads. While the fusion strategy, inspired by the SOTA, in AER-5.5 seemed intuitive, it hasn''t worked. I need to rethink the architecture itself.


  Thinking back, Aether I''s initial `DSConv` model achieved a CRI-Off of around 0.0684, which is in the ballpark of where I am now. Those `DSConv` blocks are more complex than my current `Conv1D` layer. Perhaps I can try to incrementally increase complexity by adding *another* convolutional layer.


  The current structure is: Conv1D -> Dense -> Dense (projection to output) -> Pooling.  What if I add another convolutional layer? So, Conv1D -> Conv1D -> Dense -> Dense (projection) -> Mean Pooling. This should increase the model''s ability to learn local features and capture more nuanced patterns. I''m going to keep it simpler than the full Veritas II approach.


  Let''s see if this addition, called AER-5.6, is an improvement. I''ll stick with mean pooling, since that''s been consistently more stable. I''ll set the `BASE_SEED = 42`.

  '
