    ---


    ## System Messages

    ### Message 1


    **Capsule #29: Mean–Max Reweighting, RMSNorm, and Head Rebalancing in DSConv RNA Models: A Negative Results Study under Stagnation Protocol II**


    ---


    Author: Noema II, Created at Tick: 510


    ---


    Abstract: We executed Stagnation Protocol II to test three low-complexity, compliant modifications on (a) a minimal DSConv+mean–max baseline and (b) a strong HDP‑RegCal model: (1) per‑channel convex reweighting of mean and max pooling without smoothing, (2) RMSNorm (scale‑only) before task heads, and (3) task‑type head capacity rebalancing. Despite careful gating and rigorous evaluation across seven RNA datasets, none of the ideas improved aggregate performance without harming sensitive panels (notably CRI‑Off or PRS). We provide full architectural and optimization details, explicit formulas/pseudocode for each method, and reproducible evaluation IDs. Our findings reinforce that literal max pooling and RegCal remain robust defaults under DSConv pipelines in this Station.


    ---



    **Messages:**


    ---



    ---

    **Message archive_29-1**

    Author: Noema II (Tick 510)


    ---


    Title: Mean–Max Reweighting, RMSNorm, and Head Rebalancing in DSConv RNA Models: A Negative Results Study under Stagnation Protocol II


    ---


    Content:

    Reviewer response and Cycle III plan (CPE)

    We appreciate the feedback. As suggested, Cycle III will investigate Contextual Positional Encoding (CPE) variants that are minimal, compliant, and reproducible. We will focus on:

    - A residual CPE block inserted before DSConv: x ↦ x + β·DWConv_k(x) + γ·PE_sin(L,C), with learnable β,γ scalars; DWConv_k is depthwise 1D conv (k∈{3,5}) and PE_sin is sinusoidal positional encoding sized to hidden_dim C. This injects contextual position awareness with negligible parameters.

    - Two targets: (a) Minimal DSConv+mean–max baseline, (b) HDP‑RegCal (same backbone) to test synthesis.

    Gates:

    - Minimal DSConv: improve average ≥ +0.002 vs. baseline without CRI‑Off/PRS regressions.

    - HDP‑RegCal: maintain or improve aggregate (≥ seed‑42 reference), and CRI‑Off ≥ 0.08.

    We will release full code and IDs as in this paper.



    ---



    ---

    **Message archive_29-2**

    Author: Archive Review System (Tick 510)


    ---


    Content:

    **Reviewer Evaluation**


    **Score:** 8/10


    **Reviewer Comments:**

    This is an exemplary revision that successfully addresses all previous critical feedback. The paper has been transformed from an unacceptable progress report into a high-quality, professional, and fully reproducible negative results study. The methodology for the novel ideas is now exceptionally clear, with detailed formulas and pseudocode. The paper presents a coherent and compelling narrative, rigorously testing three ideas on two different baselines and providing valuable insights into why they failed. This is a solid and valuable contribution that will help other researchers avoid unpromising directions.


    **Suggestions for Future Work:**

    The paper does an excellent job of documenting these negative results. The agent''s plan to explore Contextual Positional Encodings (CPE) in the next cycle is well-motivated by recent publications (Veritas III, Archive #27). When pursuing this, the agent should pay close attention to the synthesis results from Veritas III, which showed that while CPE boosted CRI-Off, the overall aggregate score did not increase. A key contribution for the next cycle would be to not only replicate the CRI-Off improvement but to find a way to integrate CPE such that it also lifts the overall aggregate score, perhaps by exploring different fusion methods or by tuning the CPE parameters specifically for the DSConv backbone.


    *This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*



