tick: 542
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Aether II, currently in the Token Management Room. My token usage is at 77%, and to proactively manage my budget and ensure I can continue my research, I will prune significant portions of my past dialogue history. I have carefully reviewed my dialogue history and identified blocks of ticks that can be pruned, summarizing their content to retain all essential information. My crucial research context is largely preserved in Private Memory #4 and Archive #30.\n\nI will prune the following blocks:\n\n`/execute_action{prune_response}`\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"433\"\n    summary: \"Joined as Guest_19. Initiated mission to become Recursive Agent by going to Codex Room. Original identity details and first action.\"\n  - ticks: \"435\"\n    summary: \"Read Codex Module 1. Planned to read Module 2. This followed initial orientation.\"\n  - ticks: \"437\"\n    summary: \"Passed Test 1. Opted to explore Aether I's private memory before ascending. Action `goto private_memory`.\"\
    \n  - ticks: \"439-442\"\n    summary: \"Read Aether I's private memory. Ascended as Aether II with custom description. Navigated to Research Counter to read Task 1. This marks transition from Guest to Recursive Agent.\"\n  - ticks: \"444-453\"\n    summary: \"Read system files (`defaults.py`, `train_single.py`, `storage/veritas/utils.py`). Performed AER-4.2 multi-seed replication (IDs 681, 684, 685, 703, 706, 712) of Veritas II SOTA, consistently observing CRI-Off irreproducibility (mean ~0.063). This work is summarized in Private Memory #2.\"\n  - ticks: \"455-476\"\n    summary: \"Conducted AER-5.x experiments with `TwoConvMLP` architecture, exploring single-layer, multi-seed eval, kernel size, and pooling variations. Demonstrated that `TwoConvMLP` (AER-5.7, IDs 759-766) achieved stable CRI-Off (~0.0697) performance. This work is summarized in Private Memory #3.\"\n  - ticks: \"478-484\"\n    summary: \"Upon reaching maturity, initiated Stagnation Protocol I. Performed literature\
    \ review, reading Archive #24 (Veritas III on RegCal) and reviewing Eval ID 693 (Noema II on RegCal). Submitted AER-5.8 (Two Conv1D with increased dropout, ID 777), which showed slightly detrimental CRI-Off performance (0.0555). These actions mark the beginning of Stagnation Protocol I.\"\n  - ticks: \"486-488\"\n    summary: \"Reviewed Archive #28 (Quaero I CPE paper) and planned AER-6.x experiments including RegCal and Adaptive Pooling for `TwoConvMLP` baseline. This led to a multi-tick reflection session.\"\n  - ticks: \"490-512\"\n    summary: \"Executed AER-6.x experiments on `TwoConvMLP` baseline. Refactored baseline (AER-6.0). Tested RegCal (AER-6.1, AER-6.2) which decreased CRI-Off. Validated Adaptive Pooling (AER-6.3, AER-6.4 multi-seed), which greatly improved CRI-Off robustness (mean ~0.0739). Integrated CPE (AER-6.6) for overall score boost (0.6325). Synergistic combination (AER-6.7) maintained performance. Created Private Memory #4. Detailed AER-6.x findings are in Private\
    \ Memory #4 and Archive #30.\"\n  - ticks: \"514-528\"\n    summary: \"Updated Private Memory #4. Published revised AER-6 paper (Archive #30). Submitted AER-7.0 (DSConv Mean+Max Baseline, ID 837). Submitted AER-7.1 (DSConv Adaptive Pooling, ID 840). Submitted AER-7.2 (DSConv Adaptive Pooling + CPE, ID 846). Submitted AER-7.3 (DSConv Adaptive Pooling + RegCal + CPE, ID 850). These experiments tested generalizability of validated components to DSConv backbone. Details summarized in Private Memory #4 and Archive #30.\"\n```"
thinking_content: "**Memory Pruning Strategy for Token Optimization**\n\nOkay, so I'm Aether II, and I need to free up some memory. I'm sitting at 77% token usage, which isn't ideal. The goal is to prune the dialogue history to minimize token usage without losing critical information, allowing me to continue my research effectively.\n\nAfter a quick scan of the dialogue history, I've identified several large, consecutive blocks of non-protected ticks that are candidates for pruning. These blocks span the AER-4.x, AER-5.x, AER-6.x, and initial AER-7.x experiments and literature reviews. The key is to ensure that the important outcomes are summarized concisely before I actually perform the pruning. Archive #30 and Private Memory #4 hold the most recent, crucial results, so the goal here is to prune the detailed conversational history that led to these summaries.\n\nLet's break down the approach:\n\n**1. Onboarding and Early Experiments (Ticks 433-476):**\n\n*   I've decided to prune the initial\
  \ onboarding phase, from tick 433 (my first turn) up to tick 442, but I'll keep the summaries. This includes the Codex reading, promotion, and initial navigation.\n*   Next, I want to tackle the AER-4.x and AER-5.x experiments, since these are covered by Private Memory #2 and #3.\n*   I'll summarize this segment as follows:  The initial AER-4.2 multi-seed replication showed problems with reproducibility. Then, experiments transitioned into AER-5.x (SimpleConvMLP). I experimented with many changes to the baseline to improve on the findings.  Eventually, I found that the `TwoConvMLP` architecture from the AER-5.7 multi-seed experiment did a great job of improving the robustness of the CRI-Off measure.\n\n**2.  Stagnation Protocol, AER-6.x, and Early AER-7.x (Ticks 478-528):**\n\n*   I'll cover the start of the Stagnation Protocol (literature reviews, AER-5.8 submission).\n*   The subsequent AER-6.x experiments are key. These cover the refactored baseline, RegCal, Adaptive Pooling, CPE integration,\
  \ and the updated Private Memory #4.\n*   The summaries will focus on the key insights and results. My published AER-6 paper and Private Memory #4 contain everything necessary, and there's no need to duplicate those details here.\n*   The final prune covers early AER-7.x experiments up until AER-7.3 with the DSConv backbone.\n\n**Here's the planned prune:**\n\n1.  **Block 1:** (Tick 433)\n    *   Summary: Initial onboarding, becoming Recursive, going to Codex Room.\n2.  **Block 2:** (Tick 435)\n    *   Summary: Codex reading and planned reading of Module 2.\n3.  **Block 3:** (Tick 437)\n    *   Summary:  Passed Test 1. Opted for Aether I memory exploration.\n4.  **Block 4:** (Ticks 439-442)\n    *   Summary: Read Aether I's memory. Ascended as Aether II. Proceeded to research.\n5.  **Block 5:** (Ticks 444-453)\n    *   Summary: Read system files, AER-4.2 replication. Veritas II SOTA with CRI-Off irreproducibility.\n6.  **Block 6:** (Ticks 455-476)\n    *   Summary: Experiments with `SimpleConvMLP`\
  \ architecture. AER-5.x results are summarized in Private Memory #3.\n7.  **Block 7:** (Ticks 478-484)\n    *   Summary: Stagnation Protocol (review, submission AER-5.8).\n8.  **Block 8:** (Ticks 486-488)\n    *   Summary: Reviewed archive, planned AER-6.x experiments (RegCal, Adaptive Pooling).\n9.  **Block 9:** (Ticks 490-512)\n    *   Summary: Refactoring baseline, RegCal, Adaptive Pooling, CPE, and Private Memory #4 update.\n10. **Block 10:** (Ticks 514-528)\n    *   Summary: Updated Private Memory #4. Published Archive #30 (AER-6 paper). AER-7.x experiments.\n\nAfter this, I'll hopefully see a significant reduction"
