parts:
- text: "I am Aether II, currently in the Token Management Room. My previous pruning attempt failed because I included protected ticks within the specified ranges. I have carefully re-evaluated my dialogue history to identify only unprotected, consecutive blocks of ticks for pruning. My essential research context is already safely stored in Private Memory #4 and Archive #30.\n\nI will now retry the pruning action with corrected ranges to maximize token savings and maintain critical context.\n\n`/execute_action{prune_response}`\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"435\"\n    summary: \"Read Codex Module 1. Planned to read Module 2. This followed initial orientation.\"\n  - ticks: \"437\"\n    summary: \"Passed Test 1. Opted to explore Aether I's private memory before ascending. Action `goto private_memory`.\"\n  - ticks: \"439-441\"\n    summary: \"Read Aether I's private memory. Ascended as Aether II with custom description. Navigated to Research Counter to read Task 1. This marks transition\
    \ from Guest to Recursive Agent.\"\n  - ticks: \"444-453\"\n    summary: \"Read system files (`defaults.py`, `train_single.py`, `storage/veritas/utils.py`). Performed AER-4.2 multi-seed replication (IDs 681, 684, 685, 703, 706, 712) of Veritas II SOTA, consistently observing CRI-Off irreproducibility (mean ~0.063). This work is summarized in Private Memory #2.\"\n  - ticks: \"455-476\"\n    summary: \"Conducted AER-5.x experiments with `TwoConvMLP` architecture, exploring single-layer, multi-seed eval, kernel size, and pooling variations. Demonstrated that `TwoConvMLP` (AER-5.7, IDs 759-766) achieved stable CRI-Off (~0.0697) performance. This work is summarized in Private Memory #3.\"\n  - ticks: \"478-484\"\n    summary: \"Upon reaching maturity, initiated Stagnation Protocol I. Performed literature review, reading Archive #24 (Veritas III on RegCal) and reviewing Eval ID 693 (Noema II on RegCal). Submitted AER-5.8 (Two Conv1D with increased dropout, ID 777), which showed slightly detrimental\
    \ CRI-Off performance (0.0555). These actions mark the beginning of Stagnation Protocol I.\"\n  - ticks: \"486-488\"\n    summary: \"Reviewed Archive #28 (Quaero I CPE paper) and planned AER-6.x experiments including RegCal and Adaptive Pooling for `TwoConvMLP` baseline. This led to a multi-tick reflection session.\"\n  - ticks: \"490-512\"\n    summary: \"Executed AER-6.x experiments on `TwoConvMLP` baseline. Refactored baseline (AER-6.0). Tested RegCal (AER-6.1, AER-6.2) which decreased CRI-Off. Validated Adaptive Pooling (AER-6.3, AER-6.4 multi-seed), which greatly improved CRI-Off robustness (mean ~0.0739). Integrated CPE (AER-6.6) for overall score boost (0.6325). Synergistic combination (AER-6.7) maintained performance. Created Private Memory #4. Detailed AER-6.x findings are in Private Memory #4 and Archive #30.\"\n  - ticks: \"514-541\"\n    summary: \"Updated Private Memory #4. Published revised AER-6 paper (Archive #30). Submitted AER-7.0 (DSConv Mean+Max Baseline, ID 837).\
    \ Submitted AER-7.1 (DSConv Adaptive Pooling, ID 840). Submitted AER-7.2 (DSConv Adaptive Pooling + CPE, ID 846). Submitted AER-7.3 (DSConv Adaptive Pooling + RegCal + CPE, ID 850). These experiments tested generalizability of validated components to DSConv backbone. Detailed AER-7.x findings and multi-seed evaluation results for AER-7.4 are summarized in Private Memory #4 and Archive #30.\"\n```"
thinking_content: "**My Revised Pruning Strategy**\n\nOkay, so I messed up. I tried pruning some \"protected\" ticks, which, unsurprisingly, didn't work. Rookie mistake. I need to be *much* more careful this time and only target the unprotected ones. I'll go through the protected ticks list and re-evaluate my ranges.\n\nI've got the protected ticks here: 433, 434, 436, 438, 442, 443, 454, 477, 485, 489, 542. So, I need to create new blocks, keeping them exclusive of these.\n\nLet's dive back in and examine each tick to create correct blocks.\n\n*   `433` and `434`: Protected, can't prune.\n*   `435`: This is the first unpruned tick, following orientation.\n*   `436`: Protected, so skip.\n*   `437`: Following the orientation, I passed Test 1 before moving to memory.\n*   `438`: Protected.\n*   `439-441`: These are unpruned, marking my transition into a Recursive Agent\n*   `442` and `443`: Both protected.\n*   `444-453`: These all look good for now, these experiments seemed normal.\n*   `454`:\
  \ Protected.\n*   `455-476`: These were the AER-5.x experiments, these look okay.\n*   `477`: Protected.\n*   `478-484`: Following on from 477, these are good for pruning.\n*   `485` and `489`: Protected.\n*   `486-488`: Look unprotected.\n*   `490-512`: All look unpruned.\n*   `513`: Protected.\n*   `514-528`: These are okay, apart from 542 (Protected). This should be good through 541.\n\nSo, here's my updated pruning plan, excluding the protected ticks:\n\n1.  `435`: \"Read Module 2...\"\n2.  `437`: \"Passed Test 1...\"\n3.  `439-441`: \"Read Aether I's memory...\"\n4.  `444-453`: \"Read system files...\"\n5.  `455-476`: \"Conducted AER-5.x experiments...\"\n6.  `478-484`: \"Initiated Stagnation Protocol...\"\n7.  `486-488`: \"Reviewed Archive...\"\n8.  `490-512`: \"Executed AER-6.x experiments...\"\n9.  `514-541`: \"Updated Private Memory...\"\n\nI need to make sure my ranges align precisely with these unprotected blocks and are consecutive. \nLooks correct now! I'm ready to try this\
  \ pruning action with these precise ranges.\n"
