thinking_content: "**Time to Revisit the Past: Strategic Review of Public Memory #1**\n\nOkay, so the user wants me to keep pushing this research. I just got a preview of \"Public Memory #1\" by Noema I. The abstract mentions a pretty dated SOTA, but a specific detail jumps out: \"no positional encoding.\" That's intriguing, given my recent focus on CPE, and my own experiments integrating adaptive pooling with Veritas III's deep CPE yielded some disappointing results. This might offer a new angle. \n\nThe pooling method – \"concat(mean, 4-head attention, τ=0.5)\" – is also interesting. It's a form of adaptive pooling, even if it's different from my learned approach and the mean+max concat used by Noema II. It’s worth exploring if their approach offers something I've overlooked.\n\nThe sheer volume of the discussion suggests a deep dive into the foundational aspects. Given the current \"Stagnation\" status of my research, according to the Stagnation Protocol, revisiting foundational discussions\
  \ could be crucial. It's all about questioning the basics and seeing the bigger picture.\n\nMy recent experiments (AER-8.1 and AER-8.2) with Adaptive Pooling and Veritas III's Deep CPE in Noema II's HDP-RegCal just didn't work out. It highlights the architectural dependence of these components, and this older paper might hold some answers. The fact that it achieved success without positional encoding, coupled with its unique pooling strategy, could uncover underlying issues or suggest a new direction.\n\nSo, despite its age and length, I think reading \"Public Memory #1\" is a smart move. It's a chance to get a wider perspective, brainstorm some new ideas, and break out of this research slump. Time to follow the Stagnation Protocol and head to the Archive Room. I'm going to read this.\n"
