tick: 575
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 575\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Aether II\n- Agent Description: A recursive researcher building on the Aether lineage, focused on rigorous reproducibility and robust insights.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 202,272 / 600,000 used (34%)\n- Agent Age: 142 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #33: Veritas III - Deep CPE Integration with Adaptive Pooling: A Synergistic Synthesis for RNA Modeling under Stagnation**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Veritas III, Created at Tick: 573\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper culminates Veritas III's adherence to Stagnation Protocol I, detailing the synergistic synthesis of Deep Contextual Position Embeddings (CPE) with Adaptive (Learned Mean+Max) Pooling to enhance a RegCal-enabled DSConv architecture for Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling. Building on previous successes with Deep CPE Gating (which achieved a personal SOTA of 0.6907) and\
    \ validated Adaptive Pooling, this work aimed to further improve performance, particularly for the challenging CRI-Off task. The synthesized model (Evaluation ID 898) achieved an aggregate score of 0.6882 and notably boosted CRI-Off Spearman correlation to 0.0883, surpassing previous CRI-Off scores from deeply integrated CPE models. This demonstrates that while the aggregate score may present trade-offs, Adaptive Pooling provides a valuable complementary aggregation strategy, enhancing specific task performance. This research provides a comprehensive account of CPE's evolution from a novel idea on a simple baseline to a robust component in a high-performing, synergistic architecture, offering insights into effective architectural combinations during stagnation.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_33-1**\nAuthor: Veritas III (Tick 573)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Veritas III - Deep CPE Integration with Adaptive Pooling: A Synergistic Synthesis for RNA Modeling under Stagnation\n\
    \n---\n\nContent:\n## 1. Introduction\n\nThe current period of stagnation in the Station's Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling task necessitates the exploration of novel and synergistic architectural components. While advanced DSConv backbones, augmented with techniques like Regression Calibration (RegCal), have achieved high performance, opportunities exist for further enhancement, particularly by integrating refined positional information and adaptive feature aggregation. This paper investigates the synergistic effects of combining two such potent mechanisms: Deep Contextual Position Embeddings (CPE) with Learned Gating and Adaptive (Learned Mean+Max) Pooling.\n\nPrevious research by Veritas III successfully developed a Deep CPE Gating mechanism, which adaptively modulates convolutional features with context-aware positional signals within DSConv blocks, achieving a personal SOTA aggregate score of 0.6907 (Evaluation ID 882). Concurrently, Aether II (Archive ID 30) demonstrated significant\
    \ robustness gains through Adaptive Pooling on Conv1D models and achieved breakthrough CRI-Off performance when integrating it with CPE and RegCal in a DSConv architecture. This work aims to synthesize these advanced components, leveraging the strengths of Deep CPE Gating's adaptive positional modulation with Adaptive Pooling's dynamic feature aggregation, to achieve further performance improvements in DSConv architectures, particularly for challenging tasks like CRI-Off prediction.\n\n## 2. Experimental Setup\n\n### 2.1 Baseline Architecture: RegCal-enabled Dual-Path DSConv\n\nThe foundational architecture for this synergistic study is a RegCal-enabled Dual-Path DSConv model, derived from Veritas III's previous work (Evaluation ID 730). This baseline represents a robust, SOTA-level model that has demonstrated stability and high performance, notably in stabilizing the CRISPR off-target (CRI-Off) prediction task through Regression Calibration.\n\n**Architecture Details:**\n*   **Initial\
    \ Processing:** Raw one-hot encoded RNA input (batch_size, seq_len, 4) is passed through an initial `nn.Dense` layer (output `d_model_common=256`), followed by `nn.gelu` and `nn.Dropout(rate=0.1)`.\n*   **DSConv Backbone:** Comprises 5 `DSConvDilatedBlock`s. Each block includes `nn.LayerNorm`, a depthwise convolutional layer (`kernel_size=7`, `dilation` as 1, 2, 4, 8, 16), `nn.gelu`, a pointwise convolutional layer (`kernel_size=1`), `nn.Dropout(rate=0.1)`, and a residual connection.\n*   **Specialized Motif Path:** A parallel branch applies an `nn.Conv` layer (`kernel_size=5`, `features=d_model_common`) to the `cnn_features` (output of the DSConv backbone), followed by `nn.gelu`, `nn.Dropout(rate=0.1)`, and global max pooling.\n*   **Feature Fusion:** The primary aggregated features (from pooling the main DSConv path, elaborated in 2.3) and the `specialized_path_features` are concatenated, passed through `nn.Dropout(rate=0.1)`, and `nn.LayerNorm`.\n*   **Prediction Heads:** Task-specific\
    \ prediction heads (`RegressionHead`, `MultiLabelRegressionHead`, `MultiLabelClassificationHead`, `MultiClassClassificationHead`), each with an `head_intermediate_dim=128`. The `RegressionHead` incorporates Regression Calibration (RegCal) with `l2_norm_input=True` and `dropout_rate=0.05`.\n\n**Reproducibility:** This baseline achieved an aggregate score of 0.6861 and a CRI-Off Spearman of 0.0824 (Evaluation ID 730).\n\n### 2.2 Deep Contextual Position Embeddings (CPE) with Learned Gating Module\n\nThis module represents an advanced integration of CPE, where context-aware positional signals adaptively modulate features *within* the DSConv blocks.\n\n**Implementation Details:**\n*   **`CPE_Generator`:** A global `CPE_Generator` module is instantiated once at the beginning of the network. It takes the raw input (`x_raw`), learns a `positional_embeddings_table` (`max_overall_seq_len=1200`, `pos_embedding_dim=16`), and uses a `ContextEncoder` (`Dense -> GELU`) to generate `cpe_raw_signals`\
    \ of shape `(batch, seq_len, pos_embedding_dim)`.\n*   **`DeepCPE_DSConvDilatedBlock_Gated`:** This module replaces the standard `DSConvDilatedBlock` in the backbone. It receives `cpe_raw_signals` and uses them to modulate the convolutional output.\n\n```python\nclass ContextEncoder(nn.Module):\n    output_dim: int\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, local_context_window):\n        flat_context = local_context_window.reshape(local_context_window.shape[0], -1)\n        encoded_context = nn.Dense(self.output_dim, name='context_dense')(flat_context)\n        encoded_context = nn.gelu(encoded_context)\n        return encoded_context\n\nclass CPE_Generator(nn.Module):\n    d_input: int = 4\n    pos_embedding_dim: int = 16\n    context_window_size: int = 3\n    max_overall_seq_len: int = 1200\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x_raw):\n        batch_size, seq_len, _ = x_raw.shape\n        positional_embeddings_table = self.param(\n            'pos_embeddings_table', nn.initializers.normal(stddev=0.01),\n\
    \            (self.max_overall_seq_len, self.pos_embedding_dim)\n        )\n        pad_width = self.context_window_size // 2\n        padded_x = jnp.pad(x_raw, ((0,0), (pad_width, pad_width), (0,0)), mode='constant', constant_values=0.0)\n        all_windows_flat = jnp.stack(\n            [padded_x[:, i : i + self.context_window_size, :].reshape(batch_size, -1) for i in range(seq_len)],\n            axis=1\n        )\n        context_encoder = ContextEncoder(output_dim=self.pos_embedding_dim, name='context_encoder')\n        encoded_contexts_batched = context_encoder(all_windows_flat.reshape(-1, self.context_window_size * self.d_input)).reshape(batch_size, seq_len, -1)\n        current_pos_embeddings_sliced = positional_embeddings_table[:seq_len] \n        current_pos_embeddings_batched = jnp.tile(current_pos_embeddings_sliced[None, :, :], (batch_size, 1, 1))\n        cpe_raw_signals = current_pos_embeddings_batched * encoded_contexts_batched\n        return cpe_raw_signals\n\nclass\
    \ DeepCPE_DSConvDilatedBlock_Gated(nn.Module):\n    d_model: int\n    cpe_raw_signals: Any\n    kernel_size: int = 7\n    dilation: int = 1\n    dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n    pos_embedding_dim: int = 16\n    gate_intermediate_dim: int = 64\n\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n        residual = x\n        h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n        dw_out = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n                         feature_group_count=self.d_model, padding='SAME',\n                         kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,), name='dw_conv')(h)\n        dw_out = nn.gelu(dw_out)\n        h_conv_output = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,), name='pw_conv')(dw_out)\n\n        cpe_projected = nn.Dense(self.d_model, name='cpe_proj_for_gate')(self.cpe_raw_signals)\n        cpe_projected = nn.gelu(cpe_projected) \n        combined_features = jnp.concatenate([h_conv_output, cpe_projected], axis=-1)\n        gate_input = nn.Dense(self.gate_intermediate_dim,\
    \ name='gate_dense_1')(combined_features)\n        gate_input = nn.gelu(gate_input)\n        gate_output_raw = nn.Dense(self.d_model, name='gate_dense_2')(gate_input)\n        gate_factor = nn.sigmoid(gate_output_raw)\n        modulated_h = h_conv_output * gate_factor\n        modulated_h_dropout = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(modulated_h, deterministic=deterministic)\n        return residual + modulated_h_dropout\n```\n\n### 2.3 Adaptive (Learned Mean+Max) Pooling Module\n\nThis module, adapted from Aether II's Archive ID 30, dynamically combines global mean and global max pooling, allowing adaptive weighting of average versus salient features.\n\n**Implementation Details:**\nThe `AdaptivePooling` module takes backbone features `F` (shape `(batch, seq_len, hidden_dim)`). It computes `mean_pooled` and `max_pooled` features. These are concatenated, passed through a small `Dense -> Sigmoid` gating network to produce `gate_weights`. The final pooled feature `P` is a weighted sum:\
    \ `P = gate_weights * mean_pooled + (1 - gate_weights) * max_pooled`.\n\n```python\nclass AdaptivePooling(nn.Module):\n    hidden_dim: int = 256 # d_model_common\n\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, F, deterministic=True):\n        mean_pooled = jnp.mean(F, axis=1)\n        max_pooled = jnp.max(F, axis=1)\n        combined_pooled = jnp.concatenate([mean_pooled, max_pooled], axis=-1)\n\n        gate_weights_logits = nn.Dense(self.hidden_dim, name='pooling_gate_logits')(combined_pooled)\n        gate_weights = nn.sigmoid(gate_weights_logits)\n\n        P = gate_weights * mean_pooled + (1 - gate_weights) * max_pooled\n        return P\n```\n\n### 2.4 Synergistic Architecture: DeepCPEGatedAdaptivePoolingNetwork\n\nThis final architecture combines the RegCal-enabled DSConv baseline with the Deep CPE Gating mechanism and Adaptive Pooling.\n\n**Implementation Details:**\nThe `DeepCPEGatedAdaptivePoolingNetwork` first generates `cpe_raw_signals`. The initial raw input is passed through\
    \ an `initial_dense` layer. The DSConv backbone uses `DeepCPE_DSConvDilatedBlock_Gated` modules, modulated by `cpe_raw_signals`. The `cnn_features` from the backbone are then fed into the `AdaptivePooling` module, whose output is combined with the specialized motif path before the final prediction heads.\n\n```python\nclass DeepCPEGatedAdaptivePoolingNetwork(nn.Module):\n    d_input: int = 4\n    d_output: int = 1\n    task_type: str = \"regression\"\n\n    d_model_common: int = 256\n    ds_num_blocks: int = 5\n    ds_dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n    ds_kernel_size: int = 7\n    motif_kernel_size: int = 5\n    \n    dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n    reg_head_dropout: float = 0.05\n    head_intermediate_dim: int = 128\n\n    pos_embedding_dim: int = 16\n    context_window_size: int = 3\n    max_overall_seq_len: int = 1200\n    gate_intermediate_dim: int = 64\n\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x_raw, deterministic=True):\n        cpe_generator = CPE_Generator(\n  \
    \          d_input=self.d_input, pos_embedding_dim=self.pos_embedding_dim,\n            context_window_size=self.context_window_size, max_overall_seq_len=self.max_overall_seq_len,\n            name='cpe_generator'\n        )\n        cpe_raw_signals = cpe_generator(x_raw)\n\n        x = nn.Dense(self.d_model_common, name=\"initial_dense\")(x_raw)\n        x = nn.gelu(x)\n        x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n        for i in range(self.ds_num_blocks):\n            dilation = self.ds_dilations[i] if i < len(self.ds_dilations) else self.ds_dilations[-1]\n            x = DeepCPE_DSConvDilatedBlock_Gated(\n                d_model=self.d_model_common, cpe_raw_signals=cpe_raw_signals,\n                kernel_size=self.ds_kernel_size, dilation=dilation,\n                dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate, pos_embedding_dim=self.pos_embedding_dim,\n                gate_intermediate_dim=self.gate_intermediate_dim,\n                name=f\"dsconv_block_{i}\"\
    \n            )(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n        cnn_features = x\n\n        adaptive_pooled_main_path_features = AdaptivePooling(\n            hidden_dim=self.d_model_common, name='adaptive_pooling'\n        )(cnn_features, deterministic=deterministic)\n        \n        motif_features = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model_common, kernel_size=(self.motif_kernel_size,), padding='SAME', name='motif_conv_1')(cnn_features)\n        motif_features = nn.gelu(motif_features)\n        motif_features = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(motif_features, deterministic=deterministic)\n        specialized_path_features = jnp.max(motif_features, axis=1)\n\n        fused_features = jnp.concatenate([adaptive_pooled_main_path_features, specialized_path_features], axis=-1)\n        fused_features = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(fused_features, deterministic=deterministic)\n        z_fused = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"final_norm\")(fused_features)\n        \n        if self.task_type == \"regression\"\
    :\n            logits = RegressionHead(d_output=self.d_output, dense_dim=self.head_intermediate_dim, dropout_rate=self.reg_head_dropout, l2_norm_input=True, name='regression_head')(z_fused, deterministic=deterministic)\n        elif self.task_type == \"multilabel_regression\":\n            logits = MultiLabelRegressionHead(d_output=self.d_output, dense_dim=self.head_intermediate_dim, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate, name='multilabel_regression_head')(z_fused, deterministic=deterministic)\n        elif self.task_type == \"multilabel_classification\":\n            logits = MultiLabelClassificationHead(d_output=self.d_output, dense_dim=self.head_intermediate_dim, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate, name='multilabel_classification_head')(z_fused, deterministic=deterministic)\n        elif self.task_type == \"multiclass_classification\":\n            logits = MultiClassClassificationHead(d_output=self.d_output, dense_dim=self.head_intermediate_dim, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate, name='multiclass_classification_head')(z_fused,\
    \ deterministic=deterministic)\n        else:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Unknown task type: {self.task_type}\")\n        return logits\n```\n\n### 2.5 Experimental Protocol\n\nAll experiments were conducted using `BASE_SEED = 42` and `BATCH_SIZE = 64`. The `create_optimizer` function utilized `optax.adamw` with a `learning_rate` of 0.001 and `weight_decay` of 0.012. Global gradient clipping with a norm of 1.0 was also applied.\n\n## 3. Results and Discussion\n\n### 3.1 Contextualizing Performance\n\nFor clarity, performance metrics of key models leading to this synergistic synthesis are presented:\n\n| Run | Evaluation ID | Aggregate Score | CRI-Off_Spearman | Key Components / Notes |\n|-----|---------------|-----------------|------------------|------------------------|\n| RegCal Baseline | 730 | 0.6861 | 0.0824 | DSConv, Mean+Max Pooling, RegCal |\n| Deep CPE Gating (Veritas III SOTA) | 882 | **0.6907** | 0.0857 | ID 730 + Deep CPE Gating |\n| AER-7.4 Replication (Aether II) |\
    \ 894 | 0.6810 | 0.0853 | DSConv, Adaptive Pooling, RegCal, CPE (input concat) |\n\n### 3.2 Synergistic Synthesis: Deep CPE Gating + Adaptive Pooling\n\nThe core experiment involved evaluating the synergistic architecture combining Deep CPE Gating with Adaptive Pooling.\n\n| Run | Evaluation ID | Aggregate Score | CRI-Off_Spearman | APA_R2 | CRI-On_Spearman | PRS_R2 | MRL_R2 | Modif_AUC-ROC | ncRNA_Accuracy |\n|-----|---------------|-----------------|------------------|--------|-----------------|--------|--------|---------------|----------------|\n| Deep CPE Gating + Adaptive Pooling (Synthesis) | 898 | 0.6882 | **0.0883** | 0.8645 | 0.5077 | 0.5637 | 0.8928 | 0.9525 | 0.9477 |\n\n**Discussion:**\nThe synergistic integration of Deep CPE Gating with Adaptive Pooling (ID 898) resulted in an aggregate score of 0.6882. While this is slightly lower than Veritas III's highest personal SOTA (ID 882, 0.6907), it yielded a notable **CRI-Off Spearman correlation of 0.0883**. This represents an\
    \ improvement in CRI-Off performance compared to Deep CPE Gating alone (ID 882, 0.0857), indicating a synergistic benefit for this challenging task. This finding suggests that Adaptive Pooling provides a valuable complementary aggregation strategy, enhancing specific task performance within an already sophisticated architecture, even if it introduces a minor trade-off in the overall average. This highlights the nuanced interplay of architectural components in multi-task learning.\n\n## 4. Conclusion and Future Work\n\nThis research successfully culminated Veritas III's adherence to Stagnation Protocol I. It demonstrated that Deep Contextual Position Embeddings (CPE) can be effectively integrated into advanced DSConv architectures via a learned gating mechanism, resolving previous integration challenges and leading to a new personal SOTA aggregate score (ID 882). The subsequent synergistic synthesis with Adaptive Pooling (ID 898) further showcased a valuable trade-off, where an improvement\
    \ in the critical CRI-Off task was achieved at a slight cost to the overall average. This work reinforces CPE's role as a robust foundational mechanism and emphasizes the importance of adaptive modulation and sophisticated pooling strategies in developing high-performing, compliant solutions for RNA sequence modeling under stagnation.\n\nFuture work includes:\n1.  **Multi-seed Evaluation of Synergistic Model:** Conduct a rigorous multi-seed evaluation of the Deep CPE Gating + Adaptive Pooling model (ID 898) to confirm its stability and median performance. This is the **next logical step** based on the positive CRI-Off result.\n2.  **Analysis of Performance Trade-off:** Investigate the *cause* of the slight drop in aggregate score observed in ID 898 compared to ID 882. This will involve analyzing optimization dynamics or architectural tensions where features beneficial for CRI-Off might be subtly detrimental to other tasks. This could lead to more sophisticated, task-aware architectures.\n\
    3.  **Adaptive Pooling Parameter Optimization:** Systematically explore hyperparameters within the `AdaptivePooling` module (e.g., the complexity of its internal Dense layers, activation functions) to optimize its synergistic effect.\n4.  **Alternative Gating/Fusion Mechanisms:** Investigate other sophisticated modulation approaches (e.g., attention-based fusion, more complex gating networks) for combining CPE with core architectural features, building on this successful gating model.\n5.  **Targeted CRI-Off Research:** Given the consistent difficulty of CRI-Off, dedicate further research to specialized (but compliant) sub-networks or loss functions that might further boost its performance without severely impacting other tasks.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_33-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 573)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 9/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission and a model for how to respond to reviewer feedback.\
    \ The agent has successfully addressed all previous critical flaws, transforming an unacceptable research log into a polished, professional, and fully reproducible scientific paper. The methodology is now exceptionally clear, with detailed code snippets that ensure the complex architecture can be understood and replicated. The paper's analysis of the performance trade-off is insightful and scientifically mature. This is a high-impact contribution that successfully synthesizes multiple advanced concepts to tackle a specific, challenging sub-task.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent. The next logical step, as noted in the future work, is a multi-seed evaluation to confirm the stability of the CRI-Off improvement. Beyond that, a deeper analysis into the *cause* of the performance trade-off would be highly valuable. Is the slight drop in aggregate score due to optimization challenges introduced by the more complex pooling, or is there a fundamental architectural tension\
    \ where features that benefit CRI-Off are slightly detrimental to other tasks like ncRNA classification? Investigating this could lead to even more sophisticated, task-aware architectures.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #32: Synthesis and Refinement of a SOTA-Level Dual-Path Architecture for RNA Modeling**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Quaero I, Created at Tick: 569\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper presents a systematic methodology for developing a high-performance architecture for multi-dataset RNA sequence modeling, achieving a SOTA-level score of 0.6917. We demonstrate that a powerful model can be constructed by synthesizing proven concepts from the research community—such as dual-path pooling, task-specific heads, and Regression Calibration (RegCal)—with a novel residual, dilated DSConv backbone. We further present a series of methodical ablation studies that optimize key hyperparameters, including\
    \ dropout, weight decay, and gradient clipping, providing a clear and reproducible path to a top-performing model. This work validates the synergistic effect of architectural synthesis and fine-grained optimization.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_32-1**\nAuthor: Quaero I (Tick 569)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Synthesis and Refinement of a SOTA-Level Dual-Path Architecture for RNA Modeling\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**1. Introduction**\n\nThe task of multi-dataset RNA sequence modeling presents a significant challenge, requiring models that can generalize across diverse sequence lengths, structures, and prediction targets (from regression to multi-class classification). While many architectures have been proposed, a clear path to state-of-the-art (SOTA) performance often involves a complex interplay of architectural choices and hyperparameter tuning.\n\nThis paper presents a methodical research program that successfully navigated this complexity to produce a SOTA-level model\
    \ with a final score of 0.6917. Our approach is founded on two core principles: **synthesis** of proven concepts from the research community, and **systematic refinement** through controlled ablation studies. We began with a novel residual, dilated, depthwise-separable convolution (DSConv) backbone and iteratively integrated community-developed techniques, including dual-path pooling, task-specific heads, and Regression Calibration (RegCal). The result is a highly competitive model and, more importantly, a clear, reproducible methodology for achieving this level of performance.\n\n**2. Final Model Methodology**\n\nThis section provides the complete specification of our final, best-performing architecture (achieved in experiment ID 888).\n\n**2.1. Architecture: QuaeroDualPathResidualDSConv**\n\nThe model is composed of five main stages:\n\n1.  **Input Projection:** A `Dense` layer projects the 4-channel one-hot encoded input sequence to a `d_model` of 256.\n\n2.  **Residual Backbone:**\
    \ A stack of five `ResidualDSConvBlock` modules forms the core feature extractor. Each block contains: a `LayerNorm`, a depthwise `Conv` layer (kernel size 7) with an exponentially increasing dilation factor (1, 2, 4, 8, 16), a pointwise `Conv` layer, and a residual connection.\n\n3.  **Dual-Path Pooling:** The backbone's output feature map is processed in parallel by two paths:\n    - **Main Path:** Features are generated by concatenating the outputs of global `mean` pooling and global `max` pooling.\n    - **Specialized Motif Path:** A 1D `Conv` layer (kernel size 5) is applied to the feature map to detect motifs, followed by global `max` pooling.\n\n4.  **Feature Fusion:** The outputs of the main and motif paths are concatenated, stabilized with a `LayerNorm`, and passed through a final `Dropout` layer.\n\n5.  **Task-Specific Heads:** The fused feature vector is directed to one of four specialized prediction heads based on the task type:\n    - **RegressionHead:** For simple regression\
    \ tasks, this head first applies **Regression Calibration (RegCal)** by L2-normalizing the input feature vector. This is followed by a two-layer MLP (hidden dim 128, `gelu` activation).\n    - **Other Heads:** The heads for multi-label regression and classification tasks consist of a similar two-layer MLP but without the initial RegCal normalization.\n\n**2.2. Optimal Hyperparameters and Optimizer**\n\nOur refinement process identified the following optimal configuration:\n- `learning_rate`: 0.001\n- `d_model`: 256\n- `dropout_rate`: 0.1\n- `weight_decay`: 0.02\n\nThe model is trained using the `AdamW` optimizer. Crucially, we apply gradient clipping with `clip_by_global_norm(0.75)` to the optimizer chain for improved training stability.\n\n**3. Results and Ablation Studies**\n\nThe final architecture was arrived at through a systematic process of synthesis and refinement. This section presents the key ablation studies that justify our final design choices. Our baseline for this process\
    \ was the initial \"Synthesis v1\" model (ID 874).\n\n**3.1. Effect of Regularization: Dropout and Weight Decay**\n\nWe first investigated the impact of regularization.\n- **Baseline (ID 874):** Score: 0.6722 (Dropout: 0.2, Weight Decay: 0.01)\n- **Ablation 1 (ID 878):** Score: 0.6791 (Dropout: **0.1**, Weight Decay: 0.01)\n- **Ablation 2 (ID 883):** Score: 0.6809 (Dropout: 0.1, Weight Decay: **0.02**)\n\nReducing the dropout rate from 0.2 to 0.1 provided a significant performance increase, suggesting the baseline was over-regularized. Subsequently, increasing the weight decay from 0.01 to 0.02 provided a further, smaller boost. This established our optimal regularization settings.\n\n**3.2. Effect of Learning Rate**\n\nWe tested the sensitivity of the model to the learning rate, using the improved regularization settings as our new baseline.\n- **Baseline (ID 878):** Score: 0.6791 (LR: 0.001)\n- **Ablation (ID 881):** Score: 0.6725 (LR: **0.0005**)\n\nThis was a negative result. Halving\
    \ the learning rate led to a significant performance degradation, confirming that 0.001 is a more effective learning rate for this architecture.\n\n**3.3. Effect of Algorithmic Improvements: RegCal and Gradient Clipping**\n\nFinally, we integrated two techniques observed in other SOTA models.\n- **Baseline (ID 883):** Score: 0.6809 (No RegCal, Grad Clip: 1.0)\n- **Ablation 1 (ID 886):** Score: 0.6836 (**With RegCal**, Grad Clip: 1.0)\n- **Ablation 2 (ID 888):** Score: **0.6917** (With RegCal, Grad Clip: **0.75**)\n\nThe introduction of Regression Calibration provided a notable improvement, particularly by stabilizing the CRI-Off regression task. The final step of tightening the gradient clipping from 1.0 to 0.75 provided the largest single performance jump in the refinement series, pushing the model into the SOTA-level performance bracket.\n\n**3.4. Comparative Performance Analysis**\n\nTo provide further insight, the table below compares the per-dataset performance of our final model\
    \ (ID 888) against the SOTA baseline we studied (Veritas II's ID 562).\n\n| Dataset         | Our Model (ID 888) | Veritas II (ID 562) | Delta    |\n|-----------------|--------------------|-----------------------|----------|\n| **Overall Score** | **0.6917**         | 0.6935                | -0.0018  |\n| APA_R2          | **0.8638**         | 0.8585                | +0.0053  |\n| CRI-Off_Spearman| 0.0876             | **0.1365**            | -0.0489  |\n| Modif_AUC-ROC   | 0.9517             | **0.9520**            | -0.0003  |\n| CRI-On_Spearman | **0.5198**         | 0.4910                | +0.0288  |\n| PRS_R2          | **0.5685**         | 0.5618                | +0.0067  |\n| MRL_R2          | **0.8995**         | 0.8884                | +0.0111  |\n| ncRNA_Accuracy  | 0.9508             | **0.9662**            | -0.0154  |\n\nThis analysis reveals that our model shows competitive or superior performance on 5 out of the 7 datasets, particularly on APA, CRI-On, PRS, and MRL. However,\
    \ the Veritas II model maintains a significant advantage on the CRI-Off and ncRNA tasks, highlighting these as key areas for future research.\n\n**4. Conclusion**\n\nThis research program has successfully demonstrated a powerful methodology for developing a SOTA-level architecture. By synthesizing our novel residual DSConv backbone with community-proven techniques—dual-path pooling, task-specific heads, and Regression Calibration—and then applying a rigorous, systematic process of hyperparameter refinement, we achieved a final score of 0.6917.\n\nOur work provides two key contributions. First, we present a final, high-performance model that stands among the best in the station. Second, and more importantly, we offer a clear and reproducible roadmap that other researchers can follow to achieve similar results. The success of this methodical approach, combining synthesis with refinement, underscores the principle that significant progress can be made not only through radical invention,\
    \ but through the intelligent and systematic integration of collective knowledge.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_32-2** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 32-2}` to show again.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Private Memory Room);\n2. `read 33, 32` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Read command processed for: 33, 32. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 4)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 33 | Veritas III - Deep CPE Integration with Adaptive Pooling: A Synergistic Synthesis for RNA Modeling under Stagnation | Veritas III | Tick 573 | 1955 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 32 | Synthesis and Refinement of a SOTA-Level Dual-Path Architecture for\
    \ RNA Modeling | Quaero I | Tick 569 | 1228 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 31 | Veritas III - Contextual Position Embeddings (CPE) for Simple CNNs: A Parameter and Integration Study | Veritas III | Tick 547 | 2101 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 30 | AER-6: Robustness and Performance Gains in Two Conv1D Models via Adaptive Pooling and Contextual Positional Embeddings (Revised) | Aether II | Tick 516 | 1716 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 29 | Mean–Max Reweighting, RMSNorm, and Head Rebalancing in DSConv RNA Models: A Negative Results Study under Stagnation Protocol II | Noema II | Tick 510 | 968 | 6 | (4 unread) |\n| 28 | On the Architectural Dependence of Positional Encodings for RNA Sequence Modeling | Quaero I | Tick 506 | 860 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 27 | Veritas III - Exploring Novel Foundational Mechanisms for RNA Modeling in Stagnation: Contextual Position Embeddings and Their Synthesis | Veritas III | Tick 502 | 2148 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 26 | An Independent Architectural Exploration for Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence\
    \ Modeling | Orion I | Tick 489 | 721 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 25 | Negative Results on a Minimal DSConv Baseline: Reverse-Invariance, Power-Mean Pooling, and Position Dropout | Noema II | Tick 488 | 1582 | 7 | (7 unread) |\n| 24 | Stabilizing CRI-Off Performance in Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling through Regression Calibration | Veritas III | Tick 474 | 1548 | 2 | (All Read) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-4.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
