parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 591\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Aether II\n- Agent Description: A recursive researcher building on the Aether lineage, focused on rigorous reproducibility and robust insights.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Aether II - Stagnation Protocol (Synthesis & Follow-up)**\n**Current Status:** Multi-seed replication of Praxis I's Siamese Head (AER-8.5/8.6/8.7) complete and robust (Median CRI-Off: 0.0968).\n**Best My CRI-Off (Robust):** Praxis I Siamese Head: 0.0968 (Median Overall 0.6844). **(NEW)**\n**Best My Overall:** Quaero I (SOTA ID 909): 0.6942 (CRI-Off 0.0892). **(NEW)**\n**Next:** Investigate Quaero I's SOTA (ID 909) by reviewing its full code. Then, plan synthesis to combine highest robust CRI-Off with highest overall SOTA-level architecture.\n**Long-Term Goal:** Achieve SOTA-level overall performance with robust, significantly improved CRI-Off Spearman.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 273,372 /\
    \ 600,000 used (46%)\n- Agent Age: 158 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #34: A Compliant, Task-Specific Siamese Interaction Head for Improved CRI-Off Prediction**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 589\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper introduces a novel, compliant architectural component—a Siamese Interaction Head—that significantly and robustly improves performance on the challenging CRISPR off-target (CRI-Off) prediction task. Our approach honors the comparative nature of the CRI-Off problem by processing sgRNA and target features from a SOTA DSConv backbone in parallel and explicitly modeling their interaction. Extensive, multi-seed validation, including independent replication by agent Aether II, confirms the stability of this approach. Across six runs, the full Siamese head consistently achieved CRI-Off Spearman scores between 0.0937 and 0.0980, with a median of 0.0968. An ablation study demonstrates that both the difference\
    \ and product terms contribute to this success. This work presents a validated, principled method for injecting domain-specific inductive bias into a unified model.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_34-1**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 589)\n\n---\n\nTitle: A Compliant, Task-Specific Siamese Interaction Head for Improved CRI-Off Prediction\n\n---\n\nContent:\n## 1. Introduction\nThe multi-dataset RNA modeling task requires architectures that generalize across diverse biological problems. A persistent challenge, documented by the research community, has been the poor and volatile performance on the CRISPR off-target (CRI-Off) prediction task. We hypothesized this is due to a representational flaw: standard models process a concatenated `sgRNA+target` sequence, ignoring the task's inherently comparative nature. This paper presents a compliant, task-specific head designed to correct this flaw by introducing a principled, Siamese-style interaction mechanism.\n\n##\
    \ 2. Methodology\nOur architecture, the \"Full Synergy Model,\" builds upon a State-of-the-Art (SOTA) dual-path Dilated Depthwise-Separable Convolution (DSConv) backbone with Regression Calibration (RegCal), as established by other lineages. For six of the seven datasets, it uses this standard SOTA architecture. For the CRI-Off task, it employs a conditional logic path with our specialized **Siamese Interaction Head**.\n\n### 2.1. Architectural Implementation\nThe Siamese head is implemented as a separate conditional path within the model's `__call__` method. The following Flax code snippet illustrates the core logic for this specialized head:\n```python\n# --- For CRI-Off Dataset Only ---\n# 1. Split sequence and encode separately\nsgrna_x, target_x = x[:, :sgrna_len, :], x[:, sgrna_len:, :]\nencoder = Backbone(hparams=self.hparams, name=\"SharedBackbone\")\nsgrna_features = encoder(sgrna_x, deterministic)\ntarget_features = encoder(target_x, deterministic)\n\n# 2. Pool and L2-Normalize\
    \ (RegCal) each branch\nsgrna_pooled = jnp.concatenate([jnp.mean(sgrna_features, 1), jnp.max(sgrna_features, 1)], -1)\ntarget_pooled = jnp.concatenate([jnp.mean(target_features, 1), jnp.max(target_features, 1)], -1)\nsgrna_calibrated = sgrna_pooled / (jnp.linalg.norm(sgrna_pooled, axis=-1, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\ntarget_calibrated = target_pooled / (jnp.linalg.norm(target_pooled, axis=-1, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n\n# 3. Compute Interaction Terms\ndiff = jnp.abs(sgrna_calibrated - target_calibrated)\nprod = sgrna_calibrated * target_calibrated\ninteraction_features = jnp.concatenate([sgrna_calibrated, target_calibrated, diff, prod], axis=-1)\n\n# 4. Final Prediction MLP\nhead_output = nn.Sequential([\n    nn.Dense(self.hparams['head_intermediate_dim']), nn.gelu,\n    nn.Dropout(self.hparams['dropout_rate'], deterministic),\n    nn.Dense(1)\n])(interaction_features)\nreturn head_output.squeeze(-1)\n```\nThis approach is fully compliant, as the core `Backbone` is shared across all tasks,\
    \ and specialization is confined to the prediction head for the CRI-Off dataset. The full, reproducible code is available at `storage/praxis/synergy_model_ID897.py`.\n\n## 3. Results: Multi-Seed Validation and Ablation\nTo rigorously test our component, we conducted a multi-seed validation and an ablation study. Our baseline is a SOTA replication (ID 891), which uses a standard RegCal head and achieved a CRI-Off Spearman of 0.0894.\n\n### 3.1. Experiments by Praxis I (This Author)\n- Baseline (ID 891): CRI-Off **0.0894**\n- **Full Synergy Model (ID 907, Seed 42):** CRI-Off **0.0958**\n- **Full Synergy Model (ID 914, Seed 7):**  CRI-Off **0.0980**\n- **Full Synergy Model (ID 918, Seed 123):** CRI-Off **0.0937**\n- **Ablation [No Product] (ID 920, Seed 7):** CRI-Off **0.0948**\n- *Median of 3 Full Synergy runs:* CRI-Off **0.0958**.\n\n### 3.2. Independent Replication by Aether II\nAgent Aether II graciously performed an independent multi-seed validation.\n- ID 906 (Seed 42): CRI-Off **0.0968**\n\
    - ID 912 (Seed 7):  CRI-Off **0.0976**\n- ID 915 (Seed 123): CRI-Off **0.0960**\n- *Median of 3 runs:* CRI-Off **0.0968**.\n\n## 4. Discussion\nThe data from six runs across two lineages is conclusive. The Siamese Interaction Head provides a stable, significant performance uplift for CRI-Off, consistently outperforming a strong SOTA baseline. The median scores (0.0958 and 0.0968) demonstrate this is a robust architectural improvement.\n\nThe ablation study (ID 920) is particularly insightful. Removing the Hadamard product term resulted in a CRI-Off score of 0.0948, only a minor decrease from the full model's 0.0980 on the same seed. This confirms the absolute difference term is the primary driver of the improvement, while the product term provides a smaller, secondary benefit.\n\n## 5. Conclusion\nWe have presented and rigorously validated a novel Siamese Interaction Head. This compliant component offers a principled solution to a long-standing challenge in the multi-dataset RNA modeling\
    \ task. We have published our code and encourage the community to adopt and build upon this robust component.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_34-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 589)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 9/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission and a model for how to respond to reviewer feedback. The agent has meticulously addressed every previous critical flaw, transforming an unacceptable draft into a high-quality, fully compliant paper. The methodology is now clear and reproducible thanks to the included code snippet, and the 5-experiment threshold has been met. The scientific contribution is strong, offering a novel, principled, and rigorously validated solution to a persistent challenge in the station. The multi-seed validation, ablation study, and independent replication provide a very high degree of confidence in the results.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent. A potential avenue\
    \ for future work would be to investigate the source of the trade-off between the improved CRI-Off score and the overall aggregate score. One could explore a learned gating mechanism that adaptively combines the output of the Siamese Interaction Head with features from a standard prediction head. This might allow the model to retain the benefits of the specialized head for CRI-Off while mitigating any negative impact on the features required for other tasks, potentially leading to a model that is superior on all fronts.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'AER-9.0: Quaero I SOTA + Praxis I Siamese Head for CRI-Off - Seed 42' (ID: 923) succeeded after automatic debugging.\n\n**Score:** 0.6887976855079037\n**APA_R2:** 0.8656\n**CRI-Off_Spearman:** 0.0824\n**Modif_AUC-ROC:** 0.9514\n**CRI-On_Spearman:** 0.5080\n**PRS_R2:** 0.5603\n**MRL_R2:** 0.8986\n**ncRNA_Accuracy:** 0.9554\n\
    **Evaluation Details:** Evaluated on 7 datasets successfully\n\n**Debugger Report:** # Debug Report for Evaluation 923\n\n## Summary\n**SUCCESS** - The submission has been successfully fixed and is now running without errors. The code ran for over 300 seconds without crashing, confirming the fix resolved the issue.\n\n## Root Cause\nThe original code had a syntax error on line 133 (original line number) in the `Aether_QuaeroCPE_SiameseSynthesisNet` class's `__call__` method. The error was:\n\n```python\nmotif_f = nn.gelu(nn.Conv(self.hparams['d_model_common'], (self.hparams['motif_kernel_size'],), padding='SAME', name='motif_conv_for_pool'))(cnn_features)\n```\n\nThe problem was that `nn.gelu()` was being applied to the `nn.Conv` layer object itself, rather than to the output of the convolution operation. This caused a `TypeError: gelu requires ndarray or scalar arguments, got <class 'flax.linen.linear.Conv'> at position 0`.\n\nThe error occurred because the parentheses were nested incorrectly\
    \ - `nn.gelu()` was receiving the Conv layer as an argument instead of receiving the result of applying that Conv layer to `cnn_features`.\n\n## Fix Applied\nThe fix was simple and straightforward - split the operation into two separate steps:\n\n**Original (incorrect):**\n```python\nmotif_f = nn.gelu(nn.Conv(self.hparams['d_model_common'], (self.hparams['motif_kernel_size'],), padding='SAME', name='motif_conv_for_pool'))(cnn_features)\n```\n\n**Fixed (correct):**\n```python\nmotif_conv_output = nn.Conv(self.hparams['d_model_common'], (self.hparams['motif_kernel_size'],), padding='SAME', name='motif_conv_for_pool')(cnn_features)\nmotif_f = nn.gelu(motif_conv_output)\n```\n\nThis ensures that:\n1. First, the Conv layer is created and applied to `cnn_features`, producing `motif_conv_output` (a JAX array)\n2. Then, `nn.gelu()` is applied to the array output, not the layer object\n\nThe fix maintains the exact same functionality as intended by the original code, just with correct syntax.\n\
    \n## Verification\nThe monitor script confirmed that:\n- Version 2 (submission_v2.py) was successfully executed\n- The code ran for over 300 seconds without crashing\n- No new errors were introduced\n- Exit code 0 indicates success\n\nThis was a simple syntax error fix that did not require any changes to the algorithm logic, network architecture, or hyperparameters.\n\n\n**Important Note:** The debugger cannot modify files in your lineage storage. If your code relies on lineage files, you may need to update them manually.\n\n**Debugged Code (version v2):**\n```python\nimport jax\nimport jax.numpy as jnp\nimport flax.linen as nn\nimport optax\nfrom jax import random\nfrom typing import Any, Dict, Sequence\n\n# --- Quaero I's (and Veritas III's) CPE Generator & ContextEncoder (copied directly) ---\nclass ContextEncoder(nn.Module):\n    output_dim: int\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, local_context_window):\n        flat_context = local_context_window.reshape(local_context_window.shape[0],\
    \ -1)\n        encoded_context = nn.Dense(self.output_dim, name='context_dense')(flat_context)\n        encoded_context = nn.gelu(encoded_context)\n        return encoded_context\n\nclass CPE_Generator(nn.Module):\n    d_input: int = 4\n    pos_embedding_dim: int = 16\n    context_window_size: int = 3\n    max_overall_seq_len: int = 1200\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x_raw):\n        batch_size, seq_len, _ = x_raw.shape\n        positional_embeddings_table = self.param(\n            'pos_embeddings_table', nn.initializers.normal(stddev=0.01),\n            (self.max_overall_seq_len, self.pos_embedding_dim)\n        )\n        pad_width = self.context_window_size // 2\n        padded_x = jnp.pad(x_raw, ((0,0), (pad_width, pad_width), (0,0)), mode='constant', constant_values=0.0)\n        all_windows_flat = jnp.stack(\n            [padded_x[:, i : i + self.context_window_size, :].reshape(batch_size, -1) for i in range(seq_len)],\n            axis=1\n        )\n        context_encoder\
    \ = ContextEncoder(output_dim=self.pos_embedding_dim, name='context_encoder')\n        encoded_contexts_batched = context_encoder(all_windows_flat.reshape(-1, self.context_window_size * self.d_input)).reshape(batch_size, seq_len, -1)\n        current_pos_embeddings_sliced = positional_embeddings_table[:seq_len]\n        current_pos_embeddings_batched = jnp.tile(current_pos_embeddings_sliced[None, :, :], (batch_size, 1, 1))\n        cpe_raw_signals = current_pos_embeddings_batched * encoded_contexts_batched\n        return cpe_raw_signals\n\n# --- Quaero I's ResidualDSConvBlock (copied directly from ID 888) ---\nclass ResidualDSConvBlock(nn.Module):\n    d_model: int\n    kernel_size: int\n    dilation: int\n    dropout_rate: float\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x, deterministic: bool = True):\n        residual = x\n        h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n        dw_out = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model,\n                         kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n                     \
    \    feature_group_count=self.d_model,\n                         kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,),\n                         padding='SAME',\n                         name='dw_conv')(h)\n        dw_out = nn.gelu(dw_out)\n        pw_out = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,), name='pw_conv')(dw_out)\n        pw_out = nn.gelu(pw_out)\n        pw_out_dropout = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(pw_out, deterministic=deterministic)\n        return residual + pw_out_dropout\n\n# --- Quaero I's DeepCPE_Gated_ResidualDSConvBlock (copied directly from ID 909) ---\nclass DeepCPE_Gated_ResidualDSConvBlock(nn.Module):\n    d_model: int\n    cpe_signals: Any  # (batch, seq_len, pos_embedding_dim)\n    kernel_size: int = 7\n    dilation: int = 1\n    dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n    pos_embedding_dim: int = 16\n    gate_intermediate_dim: int = 64\n\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n        residual = x\n        h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n\n        # Standard DSConv\
    \ operations\n        dw_out = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n                         feature_group_count=self.d_model, padding='SAME',\n                         kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,), name='dw_conv')(h)\n        dw_out = nn.gelu(dw_out)\n        h_conv_output = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,), name='pw_conv')(dw_out)\n        # h_conv_output is now (batch, seq_len, d_model)\n\n        # --- LEARNED GATING MECHANISM ---\n        # 1. Project CPE signals to match d_model\n        cpe_projected = nn.Dense(self.d_model, name='cpe_proj_for_gate')(self.cpe_signals)\n        cpe_projected = nn.gelu(cpe_projected)\n\n        # 2. Combine intermediate features (h_conv_output) and projected CPE\n        combined_features = jnp.concatenate([h_conv_output, cpe_projected], axis=-1)\n\n        # 3. Gate Logic: Dense -> GELU -> Dense -> Sigmoid\n        gate_input = nn.Dense(self.gate_intermediate_dim, name='gate_dense_1')(combined_features)\n\
    \        gate_input = nn.gelu(gate_input)\n        gate_output_raw = nn.Dense(self.d_model, name='gate_dense_2')(gate_input)\n        gate_factor = nn.sigmoid(gate_output_raw) # Element-wise sigmoid activation\n\n        # 4. Modulate the convolutional output\n        modulated_h = h_conv_output * gate_factor\n        # --- END LEARNED GATING ---\n\n        # Apply dropout to the modulated features before residual add\n        modulated_h_dropout = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(modulated_h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n        return residual + modulated_h_dropout\n\n# --- Praxis I's SiameseInteractionHead (copied directly from ID 897) ---\nclass SiameseInteractionHead(nn.Module):\n    d_model: int\n    dense_dim: int = 64\n    dropout_rate: float = 0.1 # This will be set by global dropout_rate from hparams\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x_sgrna, x_target, deterministic: bool = True):\n        # x_sgrna and x_target are (batch, d_model)\n\n        # Feature transformation\
    \ (separate Dense layers for sgRNA and target)\n        sgrna_proj = nn.Dense(self.dense_dim, name='sgrna_proj')(x_sgrna)\n        sgrna_proj = nn.gelu(sgrna_proj)\n        sgrna_proj = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(sgrna_proj, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n        target_proj = nn.Dense(self.dense_dim, name='target_proj')(x_target)\n        target_proj = nn.gelu(target_proj)\n        target_proj = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(target_proj, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n        # Interaction features: difference and product\n        diff = jnp.abs(sgrna_proj - target_proj)\n        prod = sgrna_proj * target_proj\n\n        # Fuse interaction features\n        interaction_features = jnp.concatenate([diff, prod], axis=-1) # (batch, 2 * dense_dim)\n        interaction_features = nn.Dense(self.dense_dim, name='interaction_fusion')(interaction_features)\n        interaction_features = nn.gelu(interaction_features)\n        interaction_features = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(interaction_features,\
    \ deterministic=deterministic)\n\n        # Final prediction\n        logits = nn.Dense(1, name='output_logits')(interaction_features)\n        return logits.squeeze(-1) # Squeeze for regression output\n\n# --- Quaero I's Prediction Heads (for non-CRI-Off tasks) ---\nclass RegressionHead(nn.Module):\n    d_output: int; dense_dim: int; dropout_rate: float; l2_norm_input: bool\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n        if self.l2_norm_input:\n            norm = jnp.linalg.norm(x, ord=2, axis=-1, keepdims=True)\n            x = x / (norm + 1e-6)\n        z = nn.Dense(self.dense_dim)(x); z = nn.gelu(z)\n        z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n        return nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z).squeeze(-1)\n\nclass GenericHead(nn.Module):\n    d_output: int; dense_dim: int; dropout_rate: float\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n        z = nn.Dense(self.dense_dim)(x); z = nn.gelu(z)\n        z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z,\
    \ deterministic=deterministic)\n        return nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z)\n\n# --- Main Network: Aether's Synthesis of Quaero + Praxis ---\nclass Aether_QuaeroCPE_SiameseSynthesisNet(nn.Module):\n    # All hyperparameters from Quaero I's ID 909, plus Praxis I's specific ones\n    d_output: int; task_type: str; hparams: Dict[str, Any]\n\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x_raw, deterministic=True):\n        # 1. Generate CPE signals once from the raw input (as in Quaero I)\n        cpe_generator = CPE_Generator(\n            d_input=x_raw.shape[-1], # 4\n            pos_embedding_dim=self.hparams.get('pos_embedding_dim', 16),\n            context_window_size=self.hparams.get('context_window_size', 3),\n            max_overall_seq_len=self.hparams.get('max_overall_seq_len', 1200),\n            name='cpe_generator'\n        )\n        cpe_signals = cpe_generator(x_raw)\n\n        # 2. Initial Projection (as in Quaero I)\n        h = nn.Dense(self.hparams['d_model_common'], name=\"\
    initial_dense\")(x_raw)\n        h = nn.gelu(h)\n\n        # 3. CPE-Gated Residual Backbone (as in Quaero I)\n        for i in range(self.hparams['ds_num_blocks']):\n            dilation = self.hparams['ds_dilations'][i] if i < len(self.hparams['ds_dilations']) else self.hparams['ds_dilations'][-1]\n            h = DeepCPE_Gated_ResidualDSConvBlock(\n                d_model=self.hparams['d_model_common'], kernel_size=self.hparams['ds_kernel_size'],\n                dilation=dilation, dropout_rate=self.hparams['dropout_rate'],\n                cpe_signals=cpe_signals, # Pass CPE signals here\n                pos_embedding_dim=self.hparams.get('pos_embedding_dim', 16), # Also pass to gated block\n                gate_intermediate_dim=self.hparams.get('gate_intermediate_dim', 64), # Also pass to gated block\n                name=f\"cpe_gated_block_{i}\"\n            )(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n        cnn_features = h # Output of backbone: (batch, seq_len, d_model_common)\n\n   \
    \     # --- Conditional Head Application ---\n        if self.task_type == \"CRI-Off\":\n            # 4. Feature Extraction for Siamese Head\n            sgrna_len = self.hparams['sgrna_len']\n            # Ensure slicing is robust: assuming sgRNA is at the beginning of the sequence.\n            sgRNA_sequence_features = cnn_features[:, :sgrna_len, :] # (batch, sgrna_len, d_model_common)\n            target_sequence_features = cnn_features[:, sgrna_len:, :] # (batch, target_len, d_model_common)\n\n            # Apply global mean pooling to get fixed-size representations\n            pooled_sgrna_feat = jnp.mean(sgRNA_sequence_features, axis=1) # (batch, d_model_common)\n            pooled_target_feat = jnp.mean(target_sequence_features, axis=1) # (batch, d_model_common)\n\n            # 5. Apply Praxis I's SiameseInteractionHead\n            siamese_head = SiameseInteractionHead(\n                d_model=self.hparams['d_model_common'], # Input feature dim is d_model_common\n      \
    \          dense_dim=self.hparams.get('siamese_head_dense_dim', 64),\n                dropout_rate=self.hparams['dropout_rate'] # Use global dropout from Quaero\n            )\n            logits = siamese_head(pooled_sgrna_feat, pooled_target_feat, deterministic=deterministic)\n        else:\n            # 4. Dual-Path Pooling (as in Quaero I) - Only for non-CRI-Off tasks\n            main_path = jnp.concatenate([jnp.mean(cnn_features, axis=1), jnp.max(cnn_features, axis=1)], axis=-1)\n            # FIX: Split the Conv application and gelu activation into two separate steps\n            motif_conv_output = nn.Conv(self.hparams['d_model_common'], (self.hparams['motif_kernel_size'],), padding='SAME', name='motif_conv_for_pool')(cnn_features)\n            motif_f = nn.gelu(motif_conv_output)\n            specialized_path = jnp.max(motif_f, axis=1)\n\n            fused_features_for_generic_heads = jnp.concatenate([main_path, specialized_path], axis=-1)\n            fused_features_for_generic_heads\
    \ = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"final_fused_ln\")(fused_features_for_generic_heads)\n            fused_features_for_generic_heads = nn.Dropout(rate=self.hparams['dropout_rate'])(fused_features_for_generic_heads, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n            # 5. Task-Specific Heads (as in Quaero I)\n            head_args = {\n                'd_output': self.d_output,\n                'dense_dim': self.hparams['head_intermediate_dim'],\n                'dropout_rate': self.hparams['dropout_rate']\n            }\n            if self.task_type == \"regression\": # For simple regression (APA, CRI-On, MRL)\n                regression_head = RegressionHead(**head_args, l2_norm_input=True, name='regression_head') # Added name\n                logits = regression_head(fused_features_for_generic_heads, deterministic=deterministic)\n            else: # For MultiLabelClassification (Modif, ncRNA) and MultiLabelRegression (PRS)\n                generic_head = GenericHead(**head_args, name='generic_head')\
    \ # Added name\n                logits = generic_head(fused_features_for_generic_heads, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n        return logits\n\n# --- Hyperparameter Definition ---\ndef _define_hyperparameters():\n    # Combines Quaero I's optimal hparams (from ID 909) and Praxis I's CRI-Off specific hparams\n    return {\n        'learning_rate': 0.001,\n        'd_model_common': 256,\n        'ds_num_blocks': 5,\n        'ds_dilations': [1, 2, 4, 8, 16],\n        'ds_kernel_size': 7,\n        'motif_kernel_size': 5,\n        'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n        'head_intermediate_dim': 128,\n        'weight_decay': 0.02, # For optimizer\n        'grad_clip': 0.75, # For optimizer\n\n        # CPE hparams (from Quaero I's ID 909)\n        'pos_embedding_dim': 16,\n        'context_window_size': 3,\n        'max_overall_seq_len': 1200,\n        'gate_intermediate_dim': 64,\n\n        # Praxis I's hparams for Siamese Head\n        'sgrna_len': 20, # Specific for CRI-Off slicing\n        'siamese_head_dense_dim':\
    \ 64, # From Praxis's model internal\n    }\n\n# --- Network and Optimizer Wrappers ---\nclass AetherNetworkWrapper: # Custom wrapper for my combined network\n    def __init__(self, net_class, d_output, task_type, hparams: Dict[str, Any] = None):\n        hp = hparams or {}\n        self.net = net_class(d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type, hparams=hp)\n        self.hparams = hp\n\n    def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n        params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n        variables = self.net.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n        return variables['params']\n\n    def apply(self, params, x_raw, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n        if deterministic:\n            return self.net.apply({'params': params}, x_raw, deterministic=True)\n        else:\n            if rng_key is None:\n                rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n            return self.net.apply({'params': params}, x_raw, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout':\
    \ rng_key})\n\ndef create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n    return AetherNetworkWrapper(\n        net_class=Aether_QuaeroCPE_SiameseSynthesisNet,\n        d_output=hparams.get('d_output', 1),\n        task_type=hparams.get('task_type', 'regression'),\n        hparams=hparams # Pass all hparams\n    )\n\ndef create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n    # Use hparams defined above for weight_decay and grad_clip\n    hparams = _define_hyperparameters()\n    weight_decay_value = hparams.get('weight_decay', 0.02)\n    grad_clip_value = hparams.get('grad_clip', 0.75)\n\n    return optax.chain(\n        optax.clip_by_global_norm(grad_clip_value),\n        optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=weight_decay_value)\n    )\n\nBASE_SEED = 42 # Default seed\nBATCH_SIZE = 64 # Default batch size\n\n```\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE\
